var e=JSON.parse('[[3301,"A method is considered idempotent if a repeated call leads to the same result as the first. `PUT` and `DELETE`, for example, remain safe when sent again, because the resource\'s state does not change after them. `POST` is normally not idempotent, because every call creates a new resource. Idempotency depends on the server\'s logic rather than on the method\'s name alone.","What idempotency is\\nIdempotency is the property of an operation of giving one and the same result when it is run again.\\nHow to determine whether an HTTP method is idempotent\\n\\n- GET - idempotent (reading data does not change it).\\n\\n- PUT - idempotent (the repeated replacement of a resource leads to the same state).\\n\\n- DELETE - idempotent (a deleted resource remains deleted).\\n\\n- POST - not idempotent (every call creates something new).\\n\\n- PATCH - it depends on the implementation (it may be, it may not).\\n\\nAn example of checking the logic\\nIf a repeated request does not change the resource\'s state, the method is idempotent.\\npython\\n\\nThe result\\nIdempotent methods let you repeat requests safely, which matters for networks, for proxies and for repeated attempts after errors.","# DELETE /users/1\\n# The second call changes nothing - the resource has already been deleted."],[3302,"The HEAD method requests only the response\'s headers, without the body. The OPTIONS method tells the client which HTTP methods and capabilities the server supports. HEAD is used for quick checks of a resource\'s availability, and OPTIONS for CORS and for determining an API\'s capabilities. These methods let you save traffic and manage the interaction of the client and the server better.","HTTP provides special methods that let you obtain meta-information about a resource without downloading the full content.\\nHEAD - obtain only the response\'s headers\\nWhat HEAD does:\\n\\n- it sends a request analogous to GET,\\n\\n- but the server returns only the headers, without the response\'s body.\\n\\nWhy it is needed:\\n\\n- The check of a resource\'s presence\\n\\n- The obtaining of a file\'s size (`Content-Length`)\\n\\n- The check of the cache (`Last-Modified`, `ETag`)\\n\\n- The saving of traffic when monitoring availability\\n\\nAn example:\\nhttp\\n\\nThe response:\\n\\nOPTIONS - learn the supported methods and the server\'s capabilities\\nThe server answers with the `Allow` header and other parameters.\\nAn example:\\nhttp\\n\\nThe response:\\n\\nIt is used for:\\n\\n- CORS\\n\\n- determining an API\'s capabilities\\n\\n- the diagnosis of routes\\n\\nThe result\\nHEAD → it is needed for checking a resource without transferring the data.\\nOPTIONS → it is needed for finding out the server\'s capabilities and for CORS.","HEAD /image.png HTTP/1.1\\n\\n200 OK\\nContent-Type: image/png\\nContent-Length: 583212\\n\\nOPTIONS /api/users HTTP/1.1\\n\\nAllow: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, OPTIONS"],[3303,"HEAD is used for obtaining only the metadata, and OPTIONS for obtaining the information about the supported methods and the server\'s capabilities. GET passes the response\'s body, which can be costly in traffic and in time. GET cannot therefore be used instead of HEAD if the aim is only to check a resource\'s existence or its parameters.","HEAD and OPTIONS were created as auxiliary methods, helping clients understand the state of the server and of its resources.\\nWhy HEAD is not the same as GET\\nAlthough HEAD and GET are handled identically, GET returns a body and HEAD does not.\\nThe problems with using GET instead of HEAD:\\n\\n- An overload of traffic\\nIf the resource is heavy (a video, an image, an archive), GET will download the whole file.\\n\\n- An increase of the response time\\nGET will make the server form the full response, which takes time.\\n\\n- An undesirable side effect\\nSome GETs are considered cacheable and can be logged, interfering with the analytics.\\n\\n- HEAD is safer for frequent checks\\nMonitors, health-check systems and proxies normally use HEAD.\\n\\nWhen to use OPTIONS\\nOPTIONS is applied for:\\n\\n- CORS preflight requests\\n\\n- determining the list of the methods the server supports\\n\\n- the testing of an API\\n\\nBefore a POST request, for example, the browser can send an OPTIONS in order to check whether the operation is permitted.\\nThe result\\nGET - for obtaining the content.\\nHEAD - for obtaining the metadata.\\nOPTIONS - for checking the server\'s capabilities.\\nHEAD cannot be replaced by GET if the speed and the absence of redundant traffic matter.",null],[3304,"APIs in industrial development are built using REST\'s principles, the clear structuring of the resources, the idempotency of the methods, clear contracts, validatable data schemas and documentation (OpenAPI/Swagger).\\nIt matters to observe predictability: a single style of URL, the correct use of the HTTP methods and response codes, and a clear separation of responsibility between the application\'s layers.\\nAn API has to be stable, extensible and backwards compatible, so that the clients can work with it without unexpected breakages.\\nA well-designed API makes the development, the maintenance and the scaling of a system easier.","The industrial creation of an API is not simply the writing of a set of endpoints. It is a strict set of rules, ensuring the API\'s reliability, predictability and convenience of use in the long term.\\nThe main principles of building an API\\n1. The observance of the REST approach\\nREST defines an architectural style for the interaction of the client and the server.\\nThe main ideas:\\n\\n- the resources are available at URLs\\n\\n- the correct HTTP methods are used\\n\\n- the standard status codes\\n\\n- the API is stateless\\n\\nAn example:\\nhttp\\n\\n2. Idempotency\\nThe idempotent methods (`GET`, `PUT`, `DELETE`) have to give an identical result when they are called again.\\nWhy that matters:\\n\\n- repeated requests (repetitions from the client, retries in the networks) should not break the data\\n\\n- it makes the work of the balancing and caching systems easier\\n\\n3. Clear contract schemas\\nThe API has to describe explicitly:\\n\\n- the format of the input data\\n\\n- the format of the response\\n\\n- the possible errors\\n\\n- the mandatory and the optional fields\\n\\nThe tools:\\n\\n- Pydantic (in FastAPI)\\n\\n- Marshmallow\\n\\n- Protocol Buffers (in gRPC)\\n\\n4. Documentation and the autogeneration of specifications\\nIn industry an API is always documented; otherwise the clients will not be able to use it.\\nThe standards:\\n\\n- OpenAPI (Swagger)\\n\\n- JSON Schema\\n\\nFastAPI, for example, generates the documentation automatically.\\n5. The versioning of the API\\nIt matters to maintain backwards compatibility.\\nExamples:\\n\\n- `/api/v1/users`\\n\\n- `/api/v2/users`\\n\\n6. The handling of the errors\\nThe correct responses:\\njson\\n\\nThe codes:\\n\\n- 200/201 - a success\\n\\n- 400 - a client error\\n\\n- 500 - a server error\\n\\n7. Security\\nWhat is used:\\n\\n- JWT tokens\\n\\n- OAuth2\\n\\n- HTTPS\\n\\n- rate limiting\\n\\nConclusion\\nA good API is a predictable, stable, documented and extensible interface. By keeping to the standards of REST, to idempotency and to clear structuring, an API of an industrial level can be built.","GET /users/1\\nPOST /users\\nPUT /users/1\\nDELETE /users/1\\n\\n{\\"detail\\": \\"User not found\\"}"],[3305,"JWT authentication works like this: the user sends their login and password → the server checks the data and issues a JWT token → the client sends that token in the headers of every request → the server validates the token and determines who is performing the request.\\nJWT is convenient in that it requires no storage of sessions on the server: all of the data about the user is sewn into the token itself.\\nInside the token there are a header, a payload and a signature, which let the data be protected against forgery.","JWT (JSON Web Token) is a popular format of token for authentication in an API. It lets the data about a user be passed in a protected form, without any need to hold state on the server.\\nThe main stages of the work of JWT authentication\\n1. The user sends their login and password\\nThe client sends a POST request:\\nhttp\\n\\nThe request\'s body:\\njson\\n\\n2. The server checks the data\\nIf the login and the password are correct → the server creates a JWT token.\\n3. A JWT consists of three parts\\n\\n- the header - the signature algorithm\\n\\n- the payload - the data (the user\'s id, the roles, the lifetime)\\n\\n- the signature - the protection against forgery\\n\\nAn example of a payload:\\njson\\n\\n4. The server returns the token to the client\\nThe client saves the token and uses it in the subsequent requests.\\n5. The client sends protected requests\\nThe token is passed in a header:\\nhttp\\n\\n6. The server validates the token\\nThe server:\\n\\n- checks the signature\\n\\n- checks the expiry date `exp`\\n\\n- extracts the user_id\\n\\nIf the token is correct → the request is considered authenticated.\\nAn example of the generation of a JWT in Python\\npython\\n\\nThe advantages of JWT\\n\\n- it requires no server storage of sessions\\n\\n- it scales easily\\n\\n- it is convenient in a microservice architecture\\n\\nThe disadvantages of JWT\\n\\n- a token cannot be revoked before its term expires without additional infrastructure\\n\\n- sensitive data cannot be held inside the payload (it is not encrypted)\\n\\nConclusion\\nJWT is a convenient, scalable way of authenticating in an API. It is well suited to microservices and to stateless systems, but it requires an attentive approach to security.","POST /auth/login\\n\\n{\\"username\\": \\"alice\\", \\"password\\": \\"secret\\"}\\n\\n{\\"user_id\\": 42, \\"exp\\": 1712345678}\\n\\nAuthorization: Bearer <jwt-token>\\n\\nimport jwt\\n\\ntoken = jwt.encode({\\"user_id\\": 1}, \\"secret\\", algorithm=\\"HS256\\")"],[3306,"Prometheus is a monitoring system that collects metrics from services over HTTP, stores them as time series and lets you run powerful queries through the PromQL language.\\nIt works on the pull model: it itself polls the `/metrics` endpoints regularly, rather than waiting for the applications to send the data.\\nPrometheus integrates with Kubernetes, supports alerts through Alertmanager and is easily visualized in Grafana.\\nThat makes it the standard of monitoring for microservice and cloud systems.","Prometheus is an open monitoring and alerting system, oriented towards the collection of time-series metrics. It is widely used in modern distributed systems because of its reliability, its flexibility and its deep integration with Kubernetes.\\nThe main particulars of Prometheus\\n1. The pull model (polling)\\nPrometheus\'s main idea is that it itself polls the services regularly:\\n\\n- every N seconds it addresses `/metrics`\\n\\n- it receives the metrics in a text format\\n\\n- it saves them as time series\\n\\nSuch an approach gives advantages:\\n\\n- simple scaling\\n\\n- there is no need for a centralized sending\\n\\n- the easy discovery of new services (through Kubernetes Service Discovery, for example)\\n\\n2. The format of the metrics\\nThe metrics have the form:\\ntext\\n\\nThe structure:\\n\\n- the metric\'s name\\n\\n- a set of labels\\n\\n- the value\\n\\nThat lets the data be filtered and aggregated flexibly.\\n3. PromQL - the query language\\nPrometheus includes its own query language, letting you:\\n\\n- compute the rate of change (`rate()`)\\n\\n- aggregate the data (`sum()`, `avg()`, `max()`)\\n\\n- build complex alerting rules\\n\\nAn example:\\ntext\\n\\nWe obtain the \\"requests per second\\".\\n4. The integration with Kubernetes\\nKubernetes is able to add services as targets automatically, which makes Prometheus the de facto standard for monitoring containers.\\nThe objects used are:\\n\\n- ServiceMonitor\\n\\n- PodMonitor\\n\\n5. Alertmanager\\nA separate component of the system, which:\\n\\n- receives the alerting rules\\n\\n- sends the notifications (Slack, email, PagerDuty)\\n\\n- supports the grouping and the suppression of alerts\\n\\nAn example of a rule:\\nyaml\\n\\n6. The ecosystem of exporters\\nPrometheus can monitor everything, even systems without support for metrics:\\n\\n- node_exporter - server metrics\\n\\n- postgres_exporter - PostgreSQL\\n\\n- redis_exporter - Redis\\n\\n- blackbox_exporter - the availability of services\\n\\nThat makes the system universal.\\n7. The difference from the other systems (ELK or Zabbix, for example)\\nPrometheus focuses on metrics rather than on logs.\\nIt is optimized for high-frequency time series and works excellently in environments with dynamic services.\\nConclusion\\nPrometheus is a powerful, flexible, scalable monitoring system, built around the ideas of:\\n\\n- the pull model of collecting metrics\\n\\n- time series\\n\\n- the PromQL language\\n\\n- the autodiscovery of services (especially in Kubernetes)\\n\\n- simple, reliable alerting\\n\\nThat makes it the main tool for monitoring microservices and cloud-native infrastructure.","http_requests_total{method=\\"GET\\", status=\\"200\\"} 1523\\napp_active_users 24\\n\\nrate(http_requests_total[5m])\\n\\n- alert: HighErrorRate\\n  expr: rate(http_requests_total{status=\\"500\\"}[5m]) > 1\\n  for: 2m\\n  labels:\\n    severity: warning\\n  annotations:\\n    description: \\"Error rate is high\\""],[3307,"When creating a system from scratch, it matters to begin with the determination of the requirements, the constraints and the key scenarios. The approaches used are: from the task (top-down), from the data (data-driven), from the events (event-driven), from the domain (DDD), from an MVP. It also matters to separate out the main components, to choose an architectural style (a monolith, microservices) and to determine the SLA and the constraints. Design is an iterative process, which begins from a simple variant and gradually grows more complex.","1. The main approaches to designing a system\\nWhen beginning a new project it is worth using one architectural approach or a combination of several.\\n1.1. The top-down approach\\nThe idea:\\n\\n- from the business task → to the components → to the modules → to the details.\\n\\nWho it suits:\\n\\n- when the requirements are clear;\\n\\n- when a quick skeleton of the system is needed.\\n\\n1.2. The bottom-up approach\\nWe start from:\\n\\n- the data;\\n\\n- the existing services;\\n\\n- the infrastructure.\\n\\nIt suits companies where part of the system already exists.\\n1.3. Data-driven design\\nFor systems with large volumes of data:\\n\\n- first we design the data models;\\n\\n- then we design the API, the events, the services.\\n\\nIt works well for analytical systems, CRM, e-commerce.\\n1.4. Event-driven design\\nThe system is built around events:\\n\\n- the services react to events (Kafka, RabbitMQ);\\n\\n- weak coupling;\\n\\n- it is easy to scale.\\n\\nIt is well suited to distributed systems and complex workflows.\\n1.5. Domain-Driven Design (DDD)\\nThe definition:\\nDDD is the approach under which the architecture is built around the model of the domain and around bounded contexts.\\nWhere it works well:\\n\\n- a complex domain;\\n\\n- a lot of teams;\\n\\n- a large project with a long lifetime.\\n\\n1.6. The approach through an MVP\\nIf the aim is a quick release of the product:\\n\\n- we begin with the minimal functionality;\\n\\n- the architecture is simplified;\\n\\n- as the product grows, we move to a more complex structure.\\n\\n1.7. The important steps at the start of the design\\n\\n- determine the functional requirements;\\n\\n- determine the non-functional requirements (the SLA, the latency, the qps);\\n\\n- choose the data model;\\n\\n- choose the architectural style;\\n\\n- choose the technologies (the database, the language, the queue, the API);\\n\\n- provide for logging, monitoring and fault tolerance.\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nWhen designing a system from scratch, the approach chosen is the one that suits the task: DDD, event-driven, data-driven, top-down and others. In practice a combination is used, beginning with the requirements and ending with the architecture.",null],[3308,"At the basic level a System Design has to include: the clients, an API gateway or load balancer, the backend services, the databases, the caches, the message queues, and the logging and monitoring system. The flows of data, the main interactions and the services\' boundaries are also shown. Such a diagram helps you understand the overall architecture without redundant details of the implementation.","1. What has to be on a basic architectural diagram\\nThe aim of the basic level is to show the key blocks and interactions.\\n1.1. The clients\\n\\n- Web and mobile applications, third parties.\\n\\n- The types of interface: REST, GraphQL, gRPC.\\n\\n1.2. The point of entry\\n\\n- A load balancer - the balancing of the load.\\n\\n- An API Gateway - routing, auth, rate limit, caching.\\n\\n1.3. The main services (the backend)\\n\\n- the business logic;\\n\\n- the services can be a monolith or microservices.\\n\\n1.4. The data storages\\n\\n- relational databases (Postgres, MySQL);\\n\\n- NoSQL (MongoDB, Redis, Cassandra);\\n\\n- blob storage (S3, MinIO).\\n\\nOn the architecture the following have to be marked:\\n\\n- master/replica;\\n\\n- the caches;\\n\\n- the indexes.\\n\\n1.5. The caching layers\\n\\n- Redis/Memcached for speeding the responses up;\\n\\n- a CDN - for the static content.\\n\\n1.6. The queues and the streaming\\n\\n- Kafka, RabbitMQ, SQS;\\n\\n- the asynchronous processes, the background work.\\n\\n1.7. Observability\\n\\n- logging (ELK, Loki);\\n\\n- metrics (Prometheus);\\n\\n- tracing (Jaeger).\\n\\n1.8. The external integrations\\n\\n- payments;\\n\\n- third-party APIs;\\n\\n- notification systems.\\n\\n1.9. The flows of data\\nIn a basic diagram it is obligatory to show:\\n\\n- what goes from where to where;\\n\\n- the sequence: the client → the API → the service → the database → the response;\\n\\n- the asynchronous branches: the service → the queue → the workers.\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nA basic System Design describes the system\'s key elements: the points of entry, the services, the databases, the caches, the queues and the observability. It shows the connections between the components and the main flows of data.",null],[3309,"A high-level system design includes the stages: the analysis of the requirements, the handling of the constraints, the determination of the key components, the design of the flows of data, the choice of the technologies and the thinking through of the scaling and the fault tolerance. At that level a diagram is created, showing how the system\'s parts interact. It sets the basis for the subsequent detailed design.","A high-level system design is the fundamental stage of the development at which the system\'s main structural elements and the principles of their interaction are determined. It lets you understand the architecture of the future product without going deep into the implementation.\\n1. The analysis of the requirements and of the scenarios of use\\nBefore designing, a clear understanding has to be formed of:\\n\\n- The functional requirements:\\n\\n- which actions the user performs;\\n\\n- which data is handled;\\n\\n- which business processes have to be automated.\\n\\n- The non-functional requirements:\\n\\n- the SLA, the permissible delays, the RPS;\\n\\n- the requirements for the security;\\n\\n- the requirements for the reliability and the availability.\\n\\nThat stage sets the frame for all of the subsequent architecture.\\n2. The determination of the constraints\\nIt matters to take into account in advance:\\n\\n- the budget and the available technologies;\\n\\n- the possible constraints on the development time;\\n\\n- the requirements for the scaling;\\n\\n- the possible peaks of load;\\n\\n- the geographical distribution of the users.\\n\\nThe constraints often determine the form of the future system more strongly than the requirements do.\\n3. The determination of the system\'s key components\\nAt that stage a \\"map\\" of the services is formed, including:\\n\\n- the load balancer / API gateway\\n\\n- the backend services\\n\\n- the databases\\n\\n- the caching layers\\n\\n- the message queues\\n\\n- the workers for the background tasks\\n\\n- the external integrations\\n\\nThe task is to separate out the main blocks and to understand their responsibility.\\n4. The design of the flows of data\\nYou have to determine:\\n\\n- the synchronous calls (REST/gRPC);\\n\\n- the asynchronous flows (Kafka/RabbitMQ);\\n\\n- the direction of the movement of the data;\\n\\n- where the data is created, changed and cached;\\n\\n- which services are the sources of truth.\\n\\nA good data flow lets you avoid errors in the integration and in the duplication of the data.\\n5. The choice of the technologies\\nThe choice of the technologies has to be justified by the characteristics required:\\n\\n- SQL/NoSQL storages;\\n\\n- the caching systems;\\n\\n- the message broker;\\n\\n- the API format;\\n\\n- the requirements for the latency;\\n\\n- the team\'s capabilities.\\n\\nA wrong choice of technologies at that stage can lead to large-scale reworking in the future.\\n6. The scaling\\nThe strategy for the scaling has to be described:\\n\\n- the horizontal scaling of the services;\\n\\n- the sharding or the replication of the databases;\\n\\n- the introduction of caches (Redis, a CDN);\\n\\n- the division of the loads into hot and cold paths.\\n\\nHigh-load architectures are almost always built around horizontal scaling.\\n7. The ensuring of the fault tolerance\\nIt includes:\\n\\n- the replication of the data;\\n\\n- automatic recovery (self-healing);\\n\\n- health-check and heartbeat mechanisms;\\n\\n- retry & backoff;\\n\\n- a circuit breaker;\\n\\n- fallback strategies.\\n\\nFault tolerance is designed in advance - it is considerably harder to introduce later.\\n8. Observability\\nAny modern system is obliged to have:\\n\\n- centralized logging;\\n\\n- the monitoring of metrics;\\n\\n- distributed tracing;\\n\\n- alerting.\\n\\nWithout observability a system remains a \\"black box\\".\\nA short conclusion\\nA high-level system design includes the analysis of the requirements, the separating out of the components, the design of the interactions, the choice of the technologies, and the ensuring of the scaling, the resilience and the observability. It is the stage that sets the overall \\"skeleton\\" of the whole architecture and determines the success of the subsequent implementation.",null],[3310,"When choosing an architecture, what is taken into account is the requirements, the technical constraints, the team\'s experience, the expected load, the complexity of the domain, the budget, the security and the requirements for observability. The architecture has to be balanced - not too complex, but resilient to growth.","The choice of an architecture is a strategic decision, affecting the cost of the development, the complexity of the maintenance and the scaling of the system. Mistakes at that level are expensive, so it matters to take all of the key factors into account.\\n1. The requirements and the scenarios of the system\'s work\\n\\n- the functional requirements form the main set of services;\\n\\n- the non-functional requirements determine the constraints: the SLA, the delays, the throughput, the requirements for the security;\\n\\n- the scenarios of use help you separate out the critical paths (the hot paths).\\n\\nAll of those elements determine how distributed the system has to be and how far it has to scale.\\n2. The expected load\\nWhat is analysed here is:\\n\\n- the initial load;\\n\\n- the potential growth by 3-10 times;\\n\\n- the peak loads (Black Friday, high-traffic events);\\n\\n- the volume of the data, which will grow constantly.\\n\\nIf the load is low, a microservice zoo is not needed.\\nIf the load is very high, a monolith will become the bottleneck.\\n3. The degree of complexity of the business domain\\n\\n- Simple domains → a monolith or a modular architecture.\\n\\n- Complex domains with a great many bounded contexts → a microservice architecture or the DDD approach.\\n\\nIt is a mistake to apply microservices where a well-designed monolith is enough.\\n4. The team and the expertise\\n\\n- the possibility of maintaining the architecture chosen;\\n\\n- the experience of working with Kubernetes, Kafka, microservices, CI/CD;\\n\\n- the possibility of hiring specialists.\\n\\nIf the team cannot do microservices, a monolith is better, which can be broken up later.\\n5. The technological constraints\\nWhat has to be taken into account is:\\n\\n- the available databases;\\n\\n- the requirements for ACID/BASE;\\n\\n- the necessity of real-time handling;\\n\\n- the requirements for the delays;\\n\\n- the constraints in the infrastructure (on-prem/cloud/hybrid).\\n\\nThe choice between Kafka and RabbitMQ, for example, depends on the patterns of handling the events.\\n6. The requirements for the security\\n\\n- the isolation of the services;\\n\\n- the control of the access;\\n\\n- the protection of the data;\\n\\n- the fencing off of the critical components.\\n\\nSome architectural decisions can be dictated by the security in particular.\\n7. The budget and the cost of the maintenance\\n\\n- microservices are more expensive to service;\\n\\n- a monolith is simpler and cheaper, but worse at scaling;\\n\\n- Kubernetes requires more expensive infrastructure.\\n\\nThe architecture has to take the financial constraints into account.\\n8. Observability\\nA good architecture provides for:\\n\\n- centralized logging;\\n\\n- metrics;\\n\\n- tracing;\\n\\n- dashboards;\\n\\n- notifications.\\n\\nObservability is an obligatory part of any modern distributed solution.\\nA short conclusion\\nThe choice of an architecture depends on the load, on the requirements, on the team\'s competences, on the complexity of the domain, on the constraints of the infrastructure, on the budget and on the requirements for observability. The architecture has to be practical, adaptive and able to evolve together with the product.",null],[3311,"The services\' boundaries are determined by the domains, by the zones of responsibility, by the structure of the data and by the flows of the interactions. Every service has to have its own model of the data, a clear responsibility and minimal dependencies. It matters to avoid excessive coupling and the duplication of logic.","The determination of the services\' boundaries is one of the most important steps in building an architecture. Well-chosen boundaries create a flexible, scalable system; bad boundaries lead to chaos, to redundant coupling and to problems with the performance.\\n1. The analysis of the domain\\nThe most reliable way of separating out the services is the use of the domain\'s natural boundaries:\\n\\n- users;\\n\\n- payments;\\n\\n- the catalogue;\\n\\n- the cart;\\n\\n- orders;\\n\\n- billing.\\n\\nSuch boundaries reflect the real business processes and rarely change.\\n2. The principle of single responsibility\\nEvery component has to solve one task.\\nThat reduces the complexity and improves the isolation.\\nExamples:\\n\\n- the Auth Service - only the authentication;\\n\\n- the Catalog Service - only the information about the products;\\n\\n- the Order Service - the creation and the management of the orders.\\n\\nThe mixing of the logic of different domains breaks the system\'s resilience.\\n3. The minimization of the coupling between the services\\nThe boundaries have to be drawn so that:\\n\\n- the services communicate through an API or through events;\\n\\n- there is no shared database between several services;\\n\\n- changes in one service do not break the others.\\n\\nHigh coupling leads to cascading crashes and makes the development harder.\\n4. Data ownership\\nEvery service has to have:\\n\\n- its own storage;\\n\\n- its own tables and indexes;\\n\\n- its own source of truth.\\n\\nIf two services write into one database, that is one service divided artificially.\\n5. The flows of data and the scenarios of use\\nThe services\' boundaries can be found by analysing:\\n\\n- where the data is created;\\n\\n- where it is changed;\\n\\n- which services initiate the processes;\\n\\n- which services depend on others.\\n\\nIf the flow goes: the catalogue → the cart → the order → the billing, that is a natural sequence of services.\\n6. The balance between coarseness and fineness\\nThe problems:\\n\\n- services that are too coarse → monolithic blocks, hard to scale;\\n\\n- services that are too fine → a lot of network calls, complexity, high costs.\\n\\nA good service is an autonomous part of the system, but not one broken up too far.\\n7. The stability of the boundaries\\nThe boundaries have to be resilient:\\n\\n- they should not change often when the functionality changes;\\n\\n- they have to reflect stable business concepts.\\n\\nIf a boundary \\"drifts\\", the service will have to be rewritten.\\nA short conclusion\\nThe services\' boundaries are determined by the domains, by the responsibility, by the flows of data and by the model of data ownership. A good service is autonomous, loosely coupled, has its own storage and solves one stable business task.",null],[3312,"For scalability what is used is horizontal and vertical scaling, caching, sharding, the division of the load, message queues, a CDN and the separating out of independent services. That lets the system withstand the growth of the load, reduce the delays and preserve its stability as the volume of the data grows.","Scalability is a system\'s ability to increase its throughput and its handling of data without a degradation of the performance. Modern architectures use a combination of approaches, since one method rarely solves the problem fully.\\n1. Horizontal scaling\\nThe definition:\\nHorizontal scaling is the addition of new instances of one and the same service.\\nIt is used when:\\n\\n- the load grows in the number of requests;\\n\\n- the system is built around stateless services;\\n\\n- there is a load balancer.\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- practically unlimited growth;\\n\\n- the quick addition of capacity;\\n\\n- high fault tolerance.\\n\\nIt is normally applied to API services, workers and gateways.\\n2. Vertical scaling\\nThe definition:\\nVertical scaling is the increase of the capacity of one machine (CPU, RAM).\\nIt is applied for:\\n\\n- databases;\\n\\n- clusters requiring large computational resources;\\n\\n- legacy applications.\\n\\nThe main drawback is the limited ceiling.\\n3. Caching\\nCaching reduces the load on the database and on the other resources:\\n\\n- an application-level cache (Redis, Memcached);\\n\\n- a CDN for the static content;\\n\\n- the caching of the results of the queries;\\n\\n- the caching of frequently used data in the services\' memory.\\n\\nThe typical path:\\nthe client → the CDN → the API → Redis → the database.\\n4. Sharding\\nThe definition:\\nSharding is the division of the data into independent segments (shards), each of which is stored separately.\\nIt is applied for large tables and for heavily loaded systems:\\n\\n- social networks (the users by ranges of id);\\n\\n- e-commerce (shards by region);\\n\\n- analytical systems.\\n\\nSharding lets you distribute the load and keep the databases light.\\n5. The replication of the databases\\nReplication ensures:\\n\\n- reading from the replicas;\\n\\n- the distribution of the load;\\n\\n- fault tolerance.\\n\\nThe master + replicas architecture is especially useful for read-heavy systems.\\n6. The division of the load and the balancing\\nLoad balancers (Nginx, HAProxy, AWS ELB) distribute the requests between the services\' instances.\\nThe useful strategies:\\n\\n- round robin;\\n\\n- least connections;\\n\\n- weighted routing;\\n\\n- health checks.\\n\\n7. Message queues\\nThe use of a message broker:\\n\\n- Kafka\\n\\n- RabbitMQ\\n\\n- SQS\\n\\n- NATS\\n\\nIt lets you:\\n\\n- move the work out of the critical path;\\n\\n- handle the data asynchronously;\\n\\n- smooth the peaks of load out.\\n\\n8. Microservices and the breaking up of a monolith\\nThe division of a system into independent services improves the scalability:\\n\\n- every service scales independently;\\n\\n- the critical components receive more resources;\\n\\n- it is easier to separate the hot paths out.\\n\\n9. A CDN and edge computing\\nData held closer to the client:\\n\\n- reduces the delays;\\n\\n- reduces the load on the central servers.\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nScalability is achieved by a combination of horizontal scaling, caching, sharding, message queues, replication, balancing and the division of the services. Every method solves its own part of the task and has to be applied depending on the current bottlenecks.",null],[3313,"In distributed systems the patterns used are: load balancing, replication, sharding, CQRS, event sourcing, the circuit breaker, the saga pattern, retry/backoff, pub/sub, eventual consistency. These templates help you design resilient, reliable, scalable systems.","Distributed systems are complex because of the network delays, the failures, the unpredictability and the need for consistency. The patterns help you structure the architecture and solve the typical problems.\\n1. Load Balancing\\nThe balancing of the load distributes the requests between the nodes.\\nMost often it is used in the API layers and in the gateway.\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- a reduction of the load on one server;\\n\\n- the ability to survive the loss of nodes.\\n\\n2. Replication\\nThe definition:\\nReplication is the duplication of the data on several nodes.\\nThe variants:\\n\\n- master → replica\\n\\n- multi-master\\n\\n- quorum replication\\n\\nIt is used for speeding the reads up and for improving the availability.\\n3. Sharding\\nThe division of the data into segments.\\nIt lets you:\\n\\n- scale the databases horizontally;\\n\\n- distribute the load;\\n\\n- avoid tables that are too large.\\n\\n4. CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)\\nIt separates the operations of reading and of writing:\\n\\n- a command - a change of the state;\\n\\n- a query - the obtaining of ready data.\\n\\nIt is useful for:\\n\\n- high loads on reading;\\n\\n- complex models of data.\\n\\n5. Event Sourcing\\nThe system\'s state is held as a stream of events.\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- an audit trail;\\n\\n- the possibility of \\"winding\\" the history;\\n\\n- the simplicity of the asynchronous processes.\\n\\n6. The Saga Pattern\\nIt is used instead of distributed transactions.\\nIt breaks a business process into steps with compensating operations.\\nIt is applied in:\\n\\n- e-commerce;\\n\\n- payment systems;\\n\\n- services with mutating operations.\\n\\n7. The Circuit Breaker\\nThe pattern that prevents cascading crashes when services are unavailable.\\nIf a service is unavailable:\\n\\n- it switches the calls to it off;\\n\\n- later it tries to \\"check\\" whether it has recovered.\\n\\n8. Retry with backoff and jitter\\nThe correct strategy of repeated attempts:\\n\\n- backoff (the duration of the wait grows);\\n\\n- jitter (the randomization of the time).\\n\\nThat prevents the overloading of the service that has crashed.\\n9. Pub/Sub\\nPublish/subscribe through Kafka, RabbitMQ.\\nIt lets the services react to events without knowing about one another.\\nA short conclusion\\nWhen designing distributed systems, the patterns widely used are: sharding, replication, CQRS, event sourcing, the saga, the circuit breaker and pub/sub. These solutions make a system resilient, scalable and correct under large loads.",null],[3314,"The choice of a storage depends on the structure of the data, on the requirements for the consistency, on the speed of the operations, on the volume of the data, on the types of query, on the load and on the necessity of horizontal scaling. Depending on the task, what is chosen is SQL, NoSQL, time-series, a graph DB, blob storage or a combination of them.","The choice of a data storage is a key decision, which determines the system\'s performance, scalability and reliability. There is no universal solution - the storage has to correspond to the model of the data and to the frequency of the operations.\\n1. The analysis of the structure of the data\\nYou have to determine:\\n\\n- the format of the data (tabular, document, graph, key-value);\\n\\n- whether there are strict relationships;\\n\\n- whether transactions are needed;\\n\\n- whether the schema will change.\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n- complex relationships → SQL or a graph DB;\\n\\n- a flexible schema → MongoDB;\\n\\n- key-value → Redis.\\n\\n2. The type of load (read-heavy, write-heavy)\\n\\n- if there are more reads → replication + caches;\\n\\n- if there are more writes → partitioning + write-optimized storage;\\n\\n- if the loads are mixed → SQL with indexes or NoSQL with sharding.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n- analytics and logging → ClickHouse, Elasticsearch;\\n\\n- OLTP → Postgres, MySQL.\\n\\n3. The requirements for the consistency\\nThe choice of ACID vs BASE:\\n\\n- financial operations → ACID (Postgres);\\n\\n- large distributed systems → BASE (Cassandra);\\n\\n- eventual consistency → Kafka + NoSQL.\\n\\n4. Scaling\\nIf horizontal scalability is required:\\n\\n- Cassandra, DynamoDB, MongoDB;\\n\\n- ClickHouse;\\n\\n- distributed time-series DBs.\\n\\nIf the scaling is moderate - Postgres with replicas.\\n5. The speed of the operations\\n\\n- a low delay → Redis;\\n\\n- heavy aggregations → analytical DBMSs;\\n\\n- large blob files → object storages (S3/MinIO).\\n\\n6. The complexity of the integration and of the maintenance\\nIt matters to take into account:\\n\\n- the team\'s experience with the particular DBMS;\\n\\n- the cost of the operation;\\n\\n- the presence of monitoring tools;\\n\\n- the maturity of the drivers.\\n\\nSometimes Postgres is the better choice simply because the team knows how to work with it.\\n7. The combining of storages\\nReal systems often use several:\\n\\n- Redis for the cache;\\n\\n- Postgres for the transactions;\\n\\n- ClickHouse for the analytics;\\n\\n- S3 for the files.\\n\\nThat creates flexibility and optimality for each type of load.\\nA short conclusion\\nA storage is chosen taking into account the structure of the data, the type of load, the requirements for the consistency, the scale, the latency and the team\'s experience. Often the optimal solution turns out to be a combination of several types of database, each of which performs its own role.",null],[3315,"Fault tolerance is achieved through replication, automatic switching (failover), the balancing of the load, the isolation of the services, retry/backoff, a circuit breaker, distributed queues, self-healing and monitoring. These mechanisms let a system stay available even when individual components crash.","Fault tolerance is a key property of distributed systems, letting them carry on working in spite of the failures of nodes, network errors or overloads. It is achieved by a combination of architectural, network and infrastructural solutions.\\n1. The replication of the data\\nReplication is the main method of protection against the loss of data and against failures.\\nThe types:\\n\\n- master-replica - the writing goes to the master, the reading from the replicas;\\n\\n- multi-master - several nodes can write;\\n\\n- quorum replication - the agreement happens through a majority.\\n\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- high availability;\\n\\n- the speeding up of the reads;\\n\\n- the possibility of a failover.\\n\\n2. Failover and automatic switching\\nA failover is the automatic switching to a reserve node when the main one crashes.\\nIt can be:\\n\\n- hot (hot standby);\\n\\n- warm (warm standby);\\n\\n- cold (cold standby).\\n\\nIt is used in databases, in Kubernetes and in balancers.\\n3. Load balancing\\nThe balancing distributes the load and protects individual nodes against overload.\\nIt also performs health checks of the services and excludes the \\"dead\\" instances from the rotation.\\n4. The Circuit Breaker\\nThe definition:\\nA circuit breaker prevents cascading failures by switching the calls to unstable services off.\\nThree states:\\n\\n- closed → the calls go through;\\n\\n- open → the calls are blocked;\\n\\n- half-open → the recovery is checked.\\n\\nThat is one of the most important mechanisms in microservices.\\n5. Retry, backoff and jitter\\nThe repeated attempts have to be made with:\\n\\n- an increase of the interval (backoff);\\n\\n- a random spread (jitter).\\n\\nThat prevents avalanches of repeated requests and protects the services.\\n6. The isolation of the services and of the dependencies\\nThe methods:\\n\\n- independent databases;\\n\\n- the separation of the resource limits;\\n\\n- the restriction of the access to the critical components;\\n\\n- timeouts.\\n\\nIf one service crashes, the rest carry on working.\\n7. Message queues\\nA message broker (Kafka, RabbitMQ, SQS) lets you:\\n\\n- smooth the peaks out;\\n\\n- handle the tasks asynchronously;\\n\\n- preserve the messages during crashes.\\n\\nThrough queues it is easy to organize repeated attempts, deadlines and deferred handling.\\n8. Self-healing (Kubernetes)\\nKubernetes and the orchestrators are able to:\\n\\n- restart the containers that have crashed;\\n\\n- recreate the nodes;\\n\\n- redistribute the load;\\n\\n- perform autoscaling.\\n\\nThat is a critical part of fault-tolerant infrastructure.\\n9. Observability and quick reactions\\nFault tolerance is impossible without:\\n\\n- logs;\\n\\n- metrics;\\n\\n- alerts;\\n\\n- tracing.\\n\\nThe quick detection of a problem = less downtime.\\nA short conclusion\\nFault tolerance is ensured by a combination of replications, failover, balancing, a circuit breaker, retry/backoff, the isolation of the services, queues, automatic recovery and full observability. It is a set of practices rather than one mechanism.",null],[3316,"Observability is achieved through centralized logging, metrics, tracing, the correlation of the requests, alerting and visualization. The ELK or Loki stack, Prometheus/Grafana and Jaeger/OpenTelemetry are used. The aim is to understand the system\'s state and to find the problems quickly and precisely.","Observability is a fundamental part of a modern architecture. It lets you see what is happening in the system, localize the problems and prevent failures.\\n1. Logging\\nCentralized logging includes:\\n\\n- structured logs (JSON);\\n\\n- the levels of logging (info, warning, error, debug);\\n\\n- the collection of the logs through agents (FluentBit, Logstash);\\n\\n- the storage in Elasticsearch, Loki, OpenSearch.\\n\\nGood practice:\\n\\n- every request has to have a correlation ID;\\n\\n- the logs have to be machine-readable rather than \\"pretty\\".\\n\\n2. Metrics\\nThe metrics give aggregated indicators:\\n\\n- the latency (p50, p95, p99);\\n\\n- the number of requests;\\n\\n- the errors;\\n\\n- the load on the CPU/RAM;\\n\\n- the delays of the queues;\\n\\n- the state of the brokers;\\n\\n- the sizes of the shards.\\n\\nNormally what is used is:\\n\\n- Prometheus;\\n\\n- Grafana;\\n\\n- VictoriaMetrics.\\n\\nMetrics are useful for the early detection of a degradation.\\n3. Tracing (distributed tracing)\\nThe traces show the request\'s path through the whole system:\\n\\n- the API gateway → service 1 → service 2 → the database → Kafka → the worker.\\n\\nThe important tools:\\n\\n- Jaeger;\\n\\n- Zipkin;\\n\\n- OpenTelemetry.\\n\\nThe traces let you:\\n\\n- identify the slow places (the bottlenecks);\\n\\n- find the requests that have hung;\\n\\n- analyse the chains of calls.\\n\\n4. The correlation of the observations\\nObservability is not simply logs, metrics and tracing separately.\\nIt works when all of the parts are linked:\\n\\n- a correlation ID in all of the logs;\\n\\n- the tracing identifiers in the logs;\\n\\n- the metrics tied to the services and to the endpoints.\\n\\nThat lets you answer the questions \\"what has broken? where? why?\\" quickly.\\n5. Alerting\\nThe system has to give notice of the problems:\\n\\n- the crash of a service;\\n\\n- a growth of the 5xx errors;\\n\\n- an increased latency;\\n\\n- a degradation of the database;\\n\\n- the overflowing of the queues.\\n\\nThe tools:\\nAlertmanager, Grafana Alerts, PagerDuty.\\nThe alerts have to be:\\n\\n- specific;\\n\\n- actionable;\\n\\n- without false firings.\\n\\n6. Dashboards\\nFor the visualization what is used is:\\n\\n- Grafana;\\n\\n- Kibana;\\n\\n- DataDog.\\n\\nThe dashboards are needed for:\\n\\n- observation in real time;\\n\\n- the analysis of anomalies;\\n\\n- the control of the SLO/SLA.\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nObservability is a complex: structured logs, metrics, tracing, the correlation of the requests, alerting and visualization. That makes distributed systems transparent, manageable and resilient.",null],[3317,"The bottlenecks are identified through the analysis of the flows of data, of the load, of the speed of the operations, of the network delays, of the database\'s structure, of the points of synchronization, of the sequential operations and of the competing resources. Profiling, metrics, tracing and the modelling of the behaviour as the load grows are used.","Bottlenecks are the components that limit a system\'s throughput. Finding them is a critical step in the design and the optimization of an architecture.\\n1. The analysis of the flows of data and of the sequence of the operations\\nThe full chain of the handling has to be drawn up:\\n\\n- the client → the gateway → the services → the database → the queue → the worker.\\n\\nThe bottlenecks often appear:\\n\\n- where the data passes through a single point;\\n\\n- where the operations are performed strictly sequentially;\\n\\n- where one service knows too much about the others.\\n\\n2. The analysis of the load\\nFor every component it matters to understand:\\n\\n- the frequency of the calls (QPS/RPS);\\n\\n- the sizes of the data;\\n\\n- the peak loads;\\n\\n- the distribution of the load over time.\\n\\nThe bottlenecks often hide in the \\"hot\\" paths.\\n3. The data storages\\nThe databases are the most frequent bottleneck:\\n\\n- slow queries;\\n\\n- the absence of indexes;\\n\\n- locks;\\n\\n- the writing into a single master;\\n\\n- tables that are too large.\\n\\nWhat also matters is:\\n\\n- the number of connections;\\n\\n- the disk\'s throughput;\\n\\n- the replicas\' limits.\\n\\n4. The network delays and the throughput\\nThe problems arise:\\n\\n- between data centres;\\n\\n- with N requests between the services;\\n\\n- when passing large payloads;\\n\\n- because of the TCP queues.\\n\\nThe network is one of the main sources of unexpected degradation.\\n5. The hardware limits\\nWhat can become the bottlenecks is:\\n\\n- the CPU;\\n\\n- the RAM;\\n\\n- the network interfaces;\\n\\n- a slow disk;\\n\\n- the containers\' limits (requests/limits in Kubernetes).\\n\\nIt is especially important to track CPU throttling.\\n6. Synchronization and locks\\nThe problems:\\n\\n- transactions that hold locks;\\n\\n- global locks;\\n\\n- shared state;\\n\\n- serialization bottlenecks.\\n\\nThat leads to a sharp fall in the performance under load.\\n7. The analysis by means of observability\\nWhat is used is:\\n\\n- the metrics (the p95/p99 latency);\\n\\n- tracing (we look at where it is \\"burning\\");\\n\\n- profiling;\\n\\n- flame graphs.\\n\\nThese tools give the factual picture of the bottlenecks.\\n8. The modelling of the load\\nBefore launching a system it matters to:\\n\\n- perform load tests;\\n\\n- check the stress scenarios;\\n\\n- simulate the failure of the dependencies;\\n\\n- test different volumes of data.\\n\\nThat lets you catch the bottlenecks before the real traffic.\\nA short conclusion\\nThe bottlenecks are identified through the analysis of the flows of data, of the loads, of the storages, of the network, of the locks, of the hardware limits and of the metrics. The use of tracing, of profiling and of load testing lets you identify and remove the bottlenecks before going to production.",null],[3318,"A JWT is a token that contains the data about a user and is signed with a secret or with a private key. The client sends the token in every request; the server checks the signature and extracts the data without addressing a database. The token can have a lifetime and additional fields (roles, permissions). The drawback is that, if a token is compromised, it is hard to revoke unless there are blacklists or a short TTL. JWT is convenient for microservices and for stateless APIs.","JWT (JSON Web Token) is a compact token format that contains useful data and a cryptographic signature.\\n1. The structure of a JWT\\nA JWT consists of three parts, separated by full stops:\\n\\n- The header - the metadata (the type of token, the signature algorithm).\\n\\n- The payload - the useful load (the claims: `sub`, `exp`, `iat`, `role` and others).\\n\\n- The signature - the signature.\\n\\nThe definition:\\nA claim is a key/value pair describing the user or the token.\\nAn example of the structure:\\nJSON\\n\\n2. How the process works\\n2.1. The creation of the token\\n\\n- the user passes the authentication (login/password);\\n\\n- the server forms the payload;\\n\\n- it signs the token with a secret (HS256) or with a private key (RS256);\\n\\n- it returns the token to the client.\\n\\n2.2. The use of the token\\nThe client sends the token in a header:\\n\\n2.3. The check on the server\\nThe server:\\n\\n- decodes the header and the payload (that is ordinary Base64);\\n\\n- checks the signature;\\n\\n- checks the lifetime (`exp`);\\n\\n- extracts the data about the user.\\n\\nThe server does not need to hold sessions - the authorization is stateless.\\n3. JWT\'s problems\\n3.1. The revocation of a token\\nA JWT cannot simply be \\"deleted\\" the way a session can. The solutions:\\n\\n- a short lifetime (5-15 minutes);\\n\\n- refresh tokens;\\n\\n- blacklists (Redis).\\n\\n3.2. The size of the token\\nThe token is long → it takes up space in every request.\\n3.3. Security\\n\\n- a JWT must not be held in localStorage (an XSS vulnerability);\\n\\n- cookies with the HttpOnly and Secure flags are safer.\\n\\n4. When JWT is useful\\n\\n- microservices (stateless);\\n\\n- mobile applications;\\n\\n- SPAs (React/Vue/Angular);\\n\\n- APIs without server sessions.\\n\\n5. Conclusion\\nJWT provides stateless authorization, convenient for APIs and for distributed systems. With the right lifetimes and with the token protected, it is safe and effective.","{\\n  \\"sub\\": \\"123\\",\\n  \\"role\\": \\"admin\\",\\n  \\"exp\\": 1712345678\\n}\\n\\nAuthorization: Bearer <JWT>"],[3319,"HTTP/2 solves HTTP/1.1\'s main problem - the inefficiency of a great many parallel requests. It uses one connection and multiplexes several streams of data inside it, avoiding the blockages and saving delay. The headers are compressed, the static resources are passed faster, and the server can send data to the client in advance (server push). As a result HTTP/2 reduces the time to load pages and reduces the network overhead.","HTTP/2: why it is needed and how it works\\nHTTP/2 was created for speeding the loading of web pages up and for solving the architectural problems present in HTTP/1.1.\\n1. HTTP/1.1\'s problem\\nHTTP/1.1 uses the model:\\n\\n- one request - one connection\\n\\n- for parallelism, several TCP connections have to be opened\\n\\n- the problem of head-of-line blocking: if one request is blocked, the rest wait\\n\\n- the headers are passed without compression and are repeated every time\\n\\nThat makes the loading of pages slow, especially if the page has 100+ resources.\\n2. HTTP/2\'s main particulars\\n1. Multiplexing\\nThe definition: multiplexing is the passing of several requests and responses at the same time within one TCP connection.\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- there are no blockages\\n\\n- there is no need for multiple TCP connections\\n\\n- a reduction of the overhead\\n\\n2. A binary protocol\\nHTTP/1.1 is textual, HTTP/2 is binary.\\nThat ensures:\\n\\n- a more compact representation\\n\\n- the simplicity of the parsing\\n\\n- fewer errors\\n\\n3. The compression of the headers (HPACK)\\nHTTP/2 compresses the headers, which in HTTP/1.1 often take up kilobytes: cookies, User-Agent, Accept and so on.\\nThat reduces the traffic and speeds the delivery up.\\n4. The priorities of the streams\\nYou can state that the CSS matters more than the images, and that the critical JS matters most of all.\\n5. Server Push (the pushing of resources)\\nThe server can send files to the client in advance, without waiting for a request. For example:\\n\\n- the client has requested the HTML\\n\\n- the server \\"knows\\" that it needs the CSS\\n\\n- the server sends the CSS itself\\n\\nThat shortens the delay of the first render.\\n3. The differences from HTTP/1.1\\n\\n- HTTP/1.1 requires a lot of TCP connections, HTTP/2 only one.\\n\\n- HTTP/1.1 passes the requests sequentially, HTTP/2 in parallel inside one connection.\\n\\n- HTTP/2 compresses the headers, HTTP/1.1 does not.\\n\\n- HTTP/2 is binary and easier to parse.\\n\\n- HTTP/2 supports Server Push, HTTP/1.1 does not.\\n\\n4. Conclusion\\nHTTP/2 is a protocol of the next generation, which solves HTTP/1.1\'s bottlenecks, speeds the delivery of the resources up and reduces the delay. Its use is especially important for sites with a large number of resources.",null],[3320,"Protobuf is a binary format for the serialization of data from Google, oriented towards compactness, speed and a strict schema of the data. It is far lighter and faster than JSON, especially when passing large structures. The binary formats let you save traffic and improve the performance, but they are less readable by a human and require the generation of structures from a schema. JSON is simpler for debugging and is universal, but it is heavier to pass and to handle. Protobuf is good where the speed and the saving of resources matter.","Protobuf and a comparison of the binary formats with JSON\\nThe definition: Protocol Buffers (Protobuf) is a binary format of serialization with a description of the structure of the data through a schema (.proto), ensuring a compact, fast representation of the data.\\n1. What Protobuf is\\nProtobuf is based on several ideas:\\n\\n- A schema of the data (a .proto file)\\nIt defines the message\'s structure: the types, the fields, the index, the repeatability.\\nproto\\n\\n- The generation of code\\nOn the basis of the schema, classes are created for the various languages (PHP, Go, Java, Python).\\n\\n- Binary serialization\\nThe result is a compact set of bytes, which is cheap to encode and to decode.\\n\\nThe advantage is the strict, checkable schema and structure.\\n2. The advantages of Protobuf and of the binary formats\\n1. Performance\\n\\n- the serialization is 5-10 times faster than JSON\'s\\n\\n- the deserialization is faster, thanks to the absence of any need to parse text\\n\\n- a binary format is simpler for a machine\\n\\n2. Compactness\\n\\n- a saving of traffic, sometimes by 2-5 times\\n\\n- there are no redundant textual labels\\n\\n- it is ideal for mobile, IoT and heavily loaded services\\n\\n3. A strict schema\\n\\n- the API\'s contract is formalized\\n\\n- it is easier to maintain backwards compatibility\\n\\n- there are fewer errors when working with the types\\n\\n4. The optimization of the network load\\nIt is excellently suited to gRPC, where a low delay matters.\\n3. The disadvantages of the binary formats\\n\\n- Unreadability by a human\\nThe bytes obtained cannot simply be opened in the console and looked at, as JSON can.\\n\\n- The necessity of a schema\\nThe .proto file is obligatory for the generation of the classes; otherwise the data cannot be decoded.\\n\\n- The complexity of debugging\\nWithout tools it is hard to see an object\'s content.\\nJSON is far more convenient in that respect.\\n\\n- Limited flexibility\\nThere are no dynamic structures without a schema described in advance, for example.\\n\\n4. JSON\'s advantages\\n\\n- readability by a human\\n\\n- built-in support in every language\\n\\n- it is easy to use in a REST API\\n\\n- flexibility (there is no schema; any structure can be sent)\\n\\n5. JSON\'s disadvantages\\n\\n- a text format → more traffic\\n\\n- it serializes more slowly\\n\\n- the typing is weak (everything is strings, numbers, arrays)\\n\\n- a large overhead for the parsing\\n\\n- potentially more errors when working with structures\\n\\n6. Where to apply Protobuf\\n\\n- gRPC\\n\\n- heavily loaded APIs\\n\\n- microservices passing a lot of data\\n\\n- mobile applications\\n\\n- server-to-server interaction\\n\\n7. Conclusion\\nProtobuf is the format for the systems where the performance, the compactness and a strict structure of the data matter. JSON suits external APIs and debugging, but the binary formats are the better choice for internal, high-performance communications.","message User {\\n    int32 id = 1;\\n    string name = 2;\\n    bool is_active = 3;\\n}"],[3321,"In REST the methods most often used are GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE. GET serves to obtain data, POST to create, PUT for the full update of a resource, and PATCH for a partial one. The difference between PUT and PATCH lies in the fact that PUT replaces the whole resource entirely, while PATCH changes only the fields stated. PATCH suits partial updates, where it matters to avoid overwriting all of the data.","The HTTP methods and the difference between PUT and PATCH\\nA REST API is built on the semantics of the standard HTTP methods.\\n1. The main HTTP methods\\n\\n- GET\\n\\n- the obtaining of a resource\\n\\n- it does not change the state\\n\\n- safe and idempotent\\n\\n- POST\\n\\n- the creation of a new resource\\n\\n- the action is not idempotent\\n\\n- the server determines the final result (the id, for example)\\n\\n- PUT\\n\\n- the full update of a resource\\n\\n- idempotent\\n\\n- the client sends the entity\'s full state\\n\\n- PATCH\\n\\n- a partial update\\n\\n- not necessarily idempotent\\n\\n- only the set of changes is sent\\n\\n- DELETE\\n\\n- the deletion of a resource\\n\\n- idempotent\\n\\nIn addition the following can be used: OPTIONS, HEAD, TRACE, CONNECT.\\n2. The difference between PUT and PATCH\\nThe definition of PUT\\nPUT is the method that replaces an existing resource with its new full version.\\nAn example:\\nphp\\n\\nEven if the age has not changed, the client is obliged to pass everything.\\nIf a field is absent, the server can consider that it needs to be deleted/reset.\\nThe definition of PATCH\\nPATCH is the partial update of a resource: the client passes only the fields that have changed.\\nAn example:\\nphp\\n\\nThe other fields remain unchanged.\\n3. When to use PUT\\n\\n- the synchronization of a resource\'s full state\\n\\n- replaceable entities (a cache, settings)\\n\\n- with an idempotent API, where the repetition of a request has to give the same result\\n\\n4. When to use PATCH\\n\\n- the updating of individual fields\\n\\n- the optimization of the network traffic\\n\\n- complex or selective updates\\n\\nPATCH is better suited to most real APIs, where a partial update arrives more often than a full one.\\n5. Conclusion\\nA REST API leans on the semantics of the HTTP methods. PUT is for the full replacement of a resource, PATCH for a partial update. The difference matters when designing stable, predictable APIs.","PUT /users/10\\n{\\n    \\"name\\": \\"John\\",\\n    \\"email\\": \\"john@example.com\\",\\n    \\"age\\": 30\\n}\\n\\nPATCH /users/10\\n{\\n    \\"email\\": \\"new@example.com\\"\\n}"],[3322,"CORS is a browser security mechanism that controls which domains can send requests to your API. It helps prevent web pages accessing other people\'s services without permission. The configuration normally includes the sending of the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, `Allow-Methods` and `Allow-Headers` headers, and the handling of the OPTIONS preflight requests. The right configuration lets you work with cross-domain APIs safely.","CORS: Cross-Origin Resource Sharing\\nThe definition: CORS is the mechanism that lets a server control the access of browser clients from other domains.\\n1. The problem that CORS solves\\nBy default the browser forbids requests from one domain to another (cross-origin), in order to:\\n\\n- prevent the theft of data\\n\\n- forbid the running of undesirable requests\\n\\n- limit XSS attacks\\n\\nThat is:\\n\\nCORS is the mechanism that lets the safe types of such request be permitted.\\n2. How CORS works\\nOn a cross-origin request the browser can:\\n\\n- Make an ordinary request (a simple request)\\n\\n- Make a preflight request by the OPTIONS method\\n\\nThe preflight request\\nThe browser asks the server:\\n\\nThe server has to answer:\\n\\nIf the server does not answer with the necessary headers, the browser blocks the request.\\n3. The main CORS headers\\n\\n- Access-Control-Allow-Origin\\nIt states which domains are permitted.\\nExamples:\\n\\n- Access-Control-Allow-Methods\\nThe available methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH\\n\\n- Access-Control-Allow-Headers\\nThe list of the permitted client headers: Authorization, Content-Type, X-Requested-With\\n\\n- Access-Control-Allow-Credentials\\nIt permits cookies and authorization data to be passed.\\n\\n- Access-Control-Max-Age\\nThe time for which the preflight response is cached.\\n\\n4. A typical configuration on the server\\nIn PHP:\\nphp\\n\\n5. The best practices of the configuration\\n\\n- Do not use `*` with authorization\\nIf cookies are needed, `*` cannot be used.\\n\\n- Permit only the domains needed\\nA list of domains is better than `*`.\\n\\n- Cache the preflight\\nIn order to reduce the OPTIONS traffic.\\n\\n- Take into account that CORS works only in the browser\\nServer-to-server requests are not affected by CORS.\\n\\n6. Conclusion\\nCORS is a browser security mechanism that permits or blocks cross-origin requests. It is configured through several headers and lets you provide an API to frontends on other domains safely.","frontend.com → api.com   (forbidden by default)\\n\\nOPTIONS /api/data\\nOrigin: https://frontend.com\\nAccess-Control-Request-Method: POST\\nAccess-Control-Request-Headers: Authorization\\n\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: https://frontend.com\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Methods: POST\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization\\n\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: *\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Origin: https://frontend.com\\n\\nAccess-Control-Allow-Credentials: true\\n\\nheader(\'Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://frontend.com\');\\nheader(\'Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE\');\\nheader(\'Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Authorization, Content-Type\');"],[3323,"In REST the methods used are GET, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE. GET is applied for obtaining data, POST for creating, PUT for the full update of an entity, PATCH for a partial one, and DELETE for deleting. Every method has its own semantics and expected behaviour, which matters for building predictable APIs.","The main HTTP methods in a REST API\\nWhen designing REST it matters to use the HTTP methods correctly, since they carry a meaning.\\n1. The main methods\\n\\n- GET\\n\\n- the obtaining of data\\n\\n- it does not change the server\'s state\\n\\n- safe and idempotent\\n\\n- POST\\n\\n- the creation of a new resource\\n\\n- it is not idempotent\\n\\n- the server normally generates the id\\n\\n- PUT\\n\\n- the full update of a resource\\n\\n- idempotent (the repetition of the request gives the same result)\\n\\n- PATCH\\n\\n- the partial update of a resource\\n\\n- only the fields stated are changed\\n\\n- it is not obliged to be idempotent\\n\\n- DELETE\\n\\n- the deletion of a resource\\n\\n- idempotent (a repeated DELETE breaks nothing)\\n\\nIn addition what is used is:\\n\\n- HEAD - obtain only the headers\\n\\n- OPTIONS - the support for CORS, the preflight requests\\n\\n- PUT/POST/PATCH with `Content-Type: application/json` - the main format of the request\'s body\\n\\n2. Why it matters to observe the methods\' semantics\\n\\n- it improves the API\'s predictability\\n\\n- it makes the integrations easier\\n\\n- it gives the caches, the proxies and the browsers correct behaviour\\n\\n- it reduces the risk of errors when working with the state\\n\\n3. Conclusion\\nA REST API is built on the right semantics of the HTTP methods: GET - we read, POST - we create, PUT/PATCH - we update, DELETE - we delete. That is the basis of the API\'s correct, expected behaviour.",null],[3324,"PUT replaces the whole resource entirely and is idempotent: a repeated call will give the same result. PATCH updates only the particular fields of a resource, without overwriting the rest, and is not obliged to be idempotent. PUT suits the full recreation of an entity, PATCH a partial, more efficient update.","The difference between PUT and PATCH\\nPUT and PATCH are both used for updating a resource, but they have different semantics.\\n1. What PUT is\\nThe definition: PUT is the method that replaces an existing resource with a new state entirely.\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- a full update\\n\\n- idempotent\\n\\n- the client sends all of the entity\'s fields\\n\\nAn example:\\njson\\n\\nEven if only one field has changed, the whole object has to be sent.\\nIf some field is absent, the server can interpret that as a deletion.\\n2. What PATCH is\\nThe definition: PATCH is the method of the partial update of a resource, changing only the fields stated.\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- a partial update\\n\\n- not necessarily idempotent\\n\\n- only the fields that have changed are passed\\n\\nAn example:\\njson\\n\\nThe user\'s other data is not touched.\\n3. When to use PUT\\n\\n- the service expects the full replacement of the resource\\n\\n- it is convenient for synchronizing the data from an external system\\n\\n- work with static structures\\n\\n- it matters that a repeated request should not change the result\\n\\n4. When to use PATCH\\n\\n- a partial update\\n\\n- a saving of traffic\\n\\n- complex resources with a large number of fields\\n\\n- frequent changes of individual fields\\n\\n- an API where it matters not to touch the data that was not stated\\n\\nPATCH is met more often in real APIs because of its flexibility.\\n5. Conclusion\\nPUT is the full replacement of a resource, and PATCH a partial update. It matters to observe the difference, so that the API stays logical, predictable and in accordance with REST\'s principles.","PUT /users/10\\n{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"John\\",\\n  \\"email\\": \\"john@example.com\\",\\n  \\"age\\": 30\\n}\\n\\nPATCH /users/10\\n{\\n  \\"email\\": \\"new@example.com\\"\\n}"],[3325,"In multiplayer it matters to understand the client-server architecture, the synchronization of the state, and the work with replication and prediction. In practice the frameworks used are ones like Mirror/Netcode for GameObjects in Unity, Unreal Networking in Unreal Engine, Photon, PlayFab, LiteNetLib, ENet, or your own implementations over UDP. The experience includes the configuration of an authoritative server, the serialization of the data, the optimization of the traffic and the development of network game mechanics. The key skill is the ability to choose a framework to suit the genre and the type of game.","The main kinds of experience that people expect to hear about\\n\\n- The client-server architecture\\n\\n- An understanding of the difference between an authoritative server and peer-to-peer.\\n\\n- The ability to build a system for replicating the state.\\n\\n- Work with prediction, interpolation and reconciliation.\\n\\n- Work with network frameworks\\n\\n- Unity: Mirror, Netcode for GameObjects (NGO), Photon Pun/Fusion, MLAPI (the old name).\\n\\n- Unreal Engine: the built-in replication, RPC, `UFUNCTION(Server/Client/NetMulticast)`.\\n\\n- Custom/low-level: LiteNetLib, ENet, RakNet, simple solutions over UDP/TCP.\\n\\n- The skills applicable in any framework\\n\\n- The serialization of the data and the optimization of the packets.\\n\\n- Work with the tick rate and with delta compression.\\n\\n- The systems of authentication and the accounting of the sessions.\\n\\n- The creation of your own protocols over UDP.\\n\\n- The tools and the infrastructure\\n\\n- Docker containers for the servers.\\n\\n- Monitoring (Grafana, Prometheus).\\n\\n- A backend API (for matchmaking, for example).\\n\\nAn example of a typical implementation of a task\\n\\nWhat is most often asked in an interview\\n\\n- Can the candidate optimize the network traffic?\\n\\n- Can they explain why an authoritative server is preferable?\\n\\n- Do they understand how prediction works?\\n\\n- Have they worked with the scaling of servers?\\n\\nConclusion\\nExperience of multiplayer is not only a knowledge of a particular framework, but also an overall understanding of the network logic of games. The framework is chosen according to the genre and the requirements: for a shooter, low-level UDP; for a cooperative game, Photon; for AAA, Unreal\'s built-in system.","// A simple sending of the player\'s position over UDP\\nstruct PlayerState {\\n    float x, y, z;\\n};\\n\\nSendToServer(Serialize(state)); // a comment: inside, a compact serialization is used"],[3326,"In network games the typical mechanics are the synchronization of movement, of attacks, of health/statuses, of shooting, of the inventory, and of the interactions between the players and the environment. Often prediction, interpolation, lag compensation, server-side checks, anti-cheat validation, network animations and the replication of objects are added. It matters to be able to build the logic so that the server is authoritative and does not trust the client.","The most widespread network mechanics\\n\\n- The synchronization of movement\\n\\n- The replication of the position/velocity.\\n\\n- Client-side prediction and a server rollback.\\n\\n- The interpolation of the other players.\\n\\n- The combat logic\\n\\n- The replication of the damage.\\n\\n- The checking of the hits on the server.\\n\\n- Server-side raycasts for the weapons.\\n\\n- Lag compensation.\\n\\n- The inventory and the items\\n\\n- The server controls the picking up/dropping of the items.\\n\\n- The replication of the inventory\'s content to the other players.\\n\\n- The spawning and the deletion of objects\\n\\n- A network factory of objects.\\n\\n- The replication of activity (the opening of a door, for example).\\n\\n- The events and the RPCs\\n\\n- The passing of custom messages: the change of weapon, shooting, the activation of an ability.\\n\\n- Matchmaking and the sessions\\n\\n- The creation of sessions, the search for them and the joining of them.\\n\\n- The management of the match\'s state.\\n\\nThe high-level mechanics\\n\\n- An authoritative server with strict checks.\\n\\n- The recomputation of the state only on the server.\\n\\n- Custom serialization and delta compression.\\n\\n- Support for jumps, sliding and custom physics.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe creation of network mechanics requires an understanding of the replication, of the prediction and of the correct server-side validation. The less the client is trusted, the more reliable the game is.","// An example of the server-side check of a shot\\nServerRPC FireWeapon(Vec3 origin, Vec3 dir) {\\n    HitResult hit = ServerRaycast(origin, dir);\\n    if (hit) ApplyDamage(hit.target);\\n}"],[3327,"Prediction lets the client show the result of the input at once, without waiting for the server\'s answer. Interpolation smooths the movements of the other players out, making them fluid. Reconciliation corrects the local state when the server sends the authoritative data. Together these techniques reduce the sense of delay and make the game experience stable even at a high ping.","The definitions of the key techniques\\nThe definition:\\n`Client-side prediction` - the client predicts the result of its own actions and shows it instantly.\\nThe definition:\\n`Interpolation` - the smoothing of the other players\' positions, in order to compensate for the network delays.\\nThe definition:\\n`Reconciliation` - the comparison of the client\'s state with the server\'s and the correction of the difference.\\nHow prediction reduces the delay\\nThe client performs the actions at once:\\n\\nThe server later sends the authoritative state, and the client compares the predicted state with the real one.\\nWhen interpolation is needed\\nFor the other players the client:\\n\\n- Receives their states with a delay.\\n\\n- Saves them in a buffer.\\n\\n- Interpolates between the states, in order to avoid jerks.\\n\\nThat improves the visual smoothness.\\nHow reconciliation works\\n\\n- The client holds the history of the inputs.\\n\\n- When the server sends the player\'s state:\\n\\n- the client accepts it as correct,\\n\\n- applies all of the inputs made after that state again,\\n\\n- corrects the position.\\n\\nThat lets prediction and correctness be combined.\\nWhy those three techniques work only together\\n\\n- Prediction gives the responsiveness.\\n\\n- Interpolation gives the smoothness of the other players.\\n\\n- Reconciliation solves prediction\'s errors.\\n\\nIf one of the links is excluded, teleports, jerks or delays will appear.\\nConclusion\\nPrediction, interpolation and reconciliation are the fundamental mechanics of network games, which make the controls responsive and smooth. They are worth applying in fast games, especially where the precision of the movement matters (shooters, platformers).","// We move the player locally\\nplayer.position += inputMove * delta;\\nSendToServer(inputMove);"],[3328,"Lag compensation lets the server take the client\'s delay into account, so that the shots and the actions are fair. A server rollback is the technique in which the server winds the world\'s state back in time (to the moment of the client\'s shot), checks the hit in the old state and then restores the current state. That makes the game fair even at different pings. Such approaches are used in shooters, in fighting games and in fast PvP.","The definitions\\nThe definition:\\n`Lag compensation` is the mechanism that reduces the influence of the network delay on the precision of the player\'s actions, by correcting the server-side computations.\\nThe definition:\\n`Server rollback` is the winding back of the game\'s state by several frames, in order to run the logic in the past and check an event correctly.\\nWhy that is needed in network games\\nAt a high ping the player sees the past and the server the present.\\nIf the player shoots at the enemy\'s visible position, the server sees the enemy already in a different place, which creates unfairness.\\nLag compensation solves that problem.\\nHow a server rollback works\\n\\n- The server holds the history of the world\'s states (normally the players\' positions) for the last N milliseconds.\\n\\n- The client sends the event (a shot, for example) together with the time when it shot.\\n\\n- The server:\\n\\n- looks for the state of the world corresponding to that time;\\n\\n- rolls back to it temporarily;\\n\\n- performs the check of the hit;\\n\\n- returns to the current state.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhere lag compensation is used\\n\\n- Shooters (FPS)\\n\\n- The precision of the shots matters.\\n\\n- An example: Counter-Strike uses hitbox rollback.\\n\\n- Fighting games\\n\\n- One of the players should not have an advantage because of the ping.\\n\\n- Rollback netcode lets prediction and the rollback of frames be performed.\\n\\n- Action games with precise combat logic\\n\\n- The checking of a blow within the timings of the animations.\\n\\nThe requirements for the implementation\\n\\n- The server has to hold the history of the states (the positions, the hitboxes).\\n\\n- The clients have to send a precise timestamp.\\n\\n- The server has to be fast enough for the rollback and the recomputation.\\n\\n- The divergences in the physics during the rollback have to be minimized.\\n\\nThe side effects and the limitations\\n\\n- The abuse of a high delay can give unfair \\"late\\" hits.\\n\\n- For slow-paced games a rollback can be redundant.\\n\\n- The server has to limit the maximum acceptable ping.\\n\\nConclusion\\nLag compensation and a server rollback are critically important techniques for competitive games, letting the players\' different delays be compensated for and ensuring identical conditions. They are worth applying in fast games, where the precision of the events is the key factor.","// the server receives the shot\\nRollbackTo(timestampFromClient);\\nPerformHitScan();\\nRestoreCurrentState();"],[3329,"The experience of interacting with a backend includes work with REST APIs, WebSocket connections, authorization systems, the storage of data, matchmaking and analytics services. The frameworks used are ones like Node.js, Python FastAPI, Go, Java Spring, and also game services like PlayFab or Firebase. A developer has to be able to send requests, to subscribe to events, to work with tokens and to understand the client-backend-game-server architecture.","The typical tasks when working with a backend\\n\\n- Authorization and the accounting of the players\\n\\n- A login through tokens.\\n\\n- OAuth or third-party providers (Steam, Google, Apple).\\n\\n- The check of the client\'s authenticity before the connection to the game server.\\n\\n- Work with a REST API\\n\\n- The obtaining of the profile\'s data.\\n\\n- The updating of the progress.\\n\\n- The obtaining of the configurations (the balance, the events).\\n\\n- Monetization and the economy\\n\\n- Purchases through the backend.\\n\\n- The checking of the transactions.\\n\\n- The server-side validation of the payments.\\n\\n- Matchmaking and the management of the sessions\\n\\n- The creation of a room.\\n\\n- The choice of a server.\\n\\n- The control of the waiting time.\\n\\n- Work with WebSocket or gRPC\\n\\n- Rating lists.\\n\\n- Events in real time.\\n\\n- Notifications.\\n\\n- The integration with the game server\\n\\n- The authorization of the clients.\\n\\n- The management of the matches\' lifecycle.\\n\\n- The recording of the results and the logs.\\n\\nWhich backend frameworks are normally met\\n\\n- Node.js / NestJS - fast prototypes, live services.\\n\\n- Python FastAPI - simple, fast REST services.\\n\\n- Go Fiber/Gin - a high-performance backend.\\n\\n- Java Spring - corporate solutions.\\n\\n- .NET WebAPI - games on C#/Unity with the integration into the Microsoft ecosystem.\\n\\nAlso met are:\\n\\n- Firebase, PlayFab, AWS GameLift, Azure PlayFab, Supabase\\n\\n- They are used for authentication, for the storage of data, for leaderboards and for inventories.\\n\\nThe skills that matter in an interview\\n\\n- An understanding of how the game interacts with the backend.\\n\\n- The ability to work with the JSON/Protobuf formats.\\n\\n- A knowledge of parsing, serialization and validation.\\n\\n- A basic understanding of security: tokens, signatures, HTTPS.\\n\\n- The ability to debug network requests.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe experience of interacting with a backend matters for any online game. It includes authorization, work with an API, matchmaking, the sessions and the storage of data. The deeper the understanding of the server architecture, the easier it is to integrate the game with external services.","// An example of sending a REST request in Unity\\nvar response = await http.Get(\\"https://api.game.com/player/123\\");\\nvar data = JsonUtility.FromJson<PlayerData>(response);"],[3330,"Asynchrony is the ability to switch between tasks without blocking while one of them waits for input/output. Parallelism is the simultaneous running of tasks on different cores. Asynchronous code does not necessarily work simultaneously, but it runs efficiently thanks to the non-blocking operations. Parallelism is needed for a CPU load, asynchrony for I/O.","The definitions\\nAsynchrony is the model of running in which a task can be suspended while it waits and the control is handed over to another task.\\nParallelism is the simultaneous running of several tasks on different processor cores.\\nAsynchrony\\nIt is applied when the program waits a lot: network requests, work with files, timers.\\nThe main advantage is the absence of blockages.\\n\\n- The characteristics:\\n\\n- It uses one thread (normally).\\n\\n- The switching of the tasks happens through an event loop.\\n\\n- It is ideal for a large number of I/O operations.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\nParallelism\\nIt suits the tasks that load the CPU actively.\\n\\n- The characteristics:\\n\\n- It uses several threads or processes.\\n\\n- The running really is simultaneous.\\n\\n- It requires the synchronization of the data.\\n\\n- An example:\\n\\nThe main difference\\n\\n- Asynchrony → the switching between tasks within one thread.\\n\\n- Parallelism → simultaneous work on several cores.\\n\\n- Asynchrony saves the waiting time; parallelism speeds the computations up.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse asynchrony for network input/output, and parallelism for CPU-intensive operations.","import asyncio\\n\\nasync def fetch():\\n    await asyncio.sleep(1)  # an imitation of I/O\\n    return 42\\n\\nfrom concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor\\n\\ndef calc(x):\\n    return x * x\\n\\nwith ProcessPoolExecutor() as pool:\\n    result = pool.map(calc, range(10))"],[3331,"REST is an architectural style based on HTTP and on the handling of individual requests. WebSocket is a two-way, persistent channel of communication. REST is well suited to ordinary CRUD operations, WebSocket to chats, to streaming and to events. REST\'s principles define what an API should look like: the absence of state, the uniformity of the interfaces and a clear resource model.","The differences between REST and WebSocket\\nREST is an architectural style of interaction through HTTP request/response; WebSocket is a protocol of a two-way, persistent connection.\\n1. REST\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- Every message is a separate HTTP request.\\n\\n- The client and the server hold no state of each other.\\n\\n- It is ideal for CRUD operations and for standard APIs.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n2. WebSocket\\nThe characteristics:\\n\\n- A persistent connection between the client and the server.\\n\\n- The two-way passing of data in real time.\\n\\n- It is used for chats, for the streaming of stock-exchange data and for online games.\\n\\nAn example (Python, FastAPI):\\n\\nREST\'s key principles\\n\\n- Client-Server - the separation of the client\'s and the server\'s logic\\n\\n- Stateless - the absence of state between the requests\\n\\n- Cacheable - the possibility of caching\\n\\n- Uniform Interface - a uniform interface\\n\\n- Layered System - the server can consist of several layers\\n\\n- Code on Demand (optional) - the server can send executable code\\n\\nConclusion\\nREST is convenient for the familiar APIs, WebSocket for the tasks of real time.","GET /users/10\\n\\n@app.websocket(\\"/ws\\")\\nasync def ws_endpoint(websocket):\\n    await websocket.accept()\\n    await websocket.send_text(\\"hello\\")"],[3332,"RPS is the number of requests per second that a system can handle. It helps you understand a service\'s throughput. RPS is assessed by means of load testing, gradually increasing the number of requests and watching the response time and the number of errors. That lets you determine the limits of the performance and the bottlenecks.","The definition of RPS\\nRPS (Requests Per Second) is the metric showing the number of requests that a system is able to handle in one second.\\nIt is used for measuring the performance of web applications and of server services.\\nHow RPS is assessed\\n\\n- Load testing\\n\\n- The tools applied are: `wrk`, `ab`, `k6`, `JMeter`, `Locust`.\\n\\n- A gradual increase of the load helps you find the point of degradation.\\n\\n- The analysis of the delays (the latency)\\n\\n- The mean, the P95 and the P99 are measured.\\n\\n- The growth of the delays as the load increases is watched.\\n\\n- The assessment of the errors\\n\\n- The `5xx` statuses, the timeouts and the failures of the connections are recorded.\\n\\n- When the errors become too many, the RPS is considered reached.\\n\\n- The monitoring of the resources\\n\\n- The CPU\\n\\n- the memory\\n\\n- the disk\\n\\n- the network\\nThe bottleneck determines the ceiling of the RPS.\\n\\nAn example of a tool\\n\\nConclusion\\nRPS reflects a service\'s throughput and is assessed by load testing with an analysis of the delays and the errors.","wrk -t4 -c100 -d30s http://localhost:8000"],[3333,"An SLA is an agreed level of a service\'s quality, the percentage of the time it is available, for example. It helps formalize the obligations between teams or between suppliers of services. An SLA lets you assess a system\'s stability, take decisions about redundancy and tune the monitoring processes.","The definition of an SLA\\nAn SLA (Service Level Agreement) is a document or a metric that records the level of availability and quality of a service that the team or the supplier undertakes to maintain.\\nIt sets particular guarantees, for example: \\"the service is available 99.9% of the time\\".\\nThe main parameters of an SLA\\n\\n- The availability\\n\\n- It is stated in percentages, 99.95%, for example.\\n\\n- The response time\\n\\n- The values required for the P95, the P99.\\n\\n- The permissible level of errors\\n\\n- The maximum percentage of failures or errors.\\n\\n- The recovery time (the MTTR)\\n\\n- The maximum permissible time for the removal of an incident.\\n\\nWhy an SLA is needed\\n\\n- It defines the minimum quality of the service.\\n\\n- It establishes the expectations between the teams.\\n\\n- It helps assess the work of the operations team.\\n\\n- It determines the resources needed (redundancy, replication, monitoring).\\n\\nAn example of the wording\\n\\"The API has to be available 99.9% of the time per month; the response time to a request does not exceed 200 ms for the P95\\".\\nConclusion\\nAn SLA helps formalize the requirements for a service\'s stability and manage the quality of the work.",null],[3334,"The optimization of server applications is aimed at speeding the handling of the requests up and at reducing the consumption of the resources. It includes profiling, the removal of the bottlenecks, the optimization of the code, the right choice of algorithms, the configuration of the environment and scaling. Optimization is always done on the basis of measurements rather than of suppositions.","What optimization is\\nOptimization is the systematic improvement of an application\'s performance on the basis of measurements and of the analysis of the bottlenecks.\\nIt includes work with the code and also with the environment and the infrastructure.\\nThe main directions of the optimization\\n\\n- The optimization of the code\\n\\n- The use of efficient data structures\\n\\n- A reduction of the number of operations\\n\\n- The avoidance of redundant allocations\\n\\n- The rewriting of the heavy stretches in Cython or Rust when necessary\\n\\n- The optimization of the queries to the database\\n\\n- The indexes\\n\\n- A reduction of the number of queries\\n\\n- Caching\\n\\n- Batch operations\\n\\n- Work with the network delays\\n\\n- The use of asynchrony\\n\\n- A reduction of the number of network calls\\n\\n- A reduction of the size of the messages\\n\\n- The optimization of the memory\\n\\n- The search for leaks\\n\\n- A reduction of the allocations of objects\\n\\n- The use of `slots` or of structures\\n\\n- The optimization of the infrastructure\\n\\n- The configuration of the balancers\\n\\n- Horizontal scaling\\n\\n- Vertical scaling (CPU, RAM)\\n\\n- Caching\\n\\n- It is applied at every level: the database, the API, the external services\\n\\n- The tools: Redis, memcache, the HTTP cache\\n\\nAn example of the optimization of the queries\\n\\nConclusion\\nOptimization is the process of improving the performance on the basis of data rather than of intuition.","# bad - two queries\\nuser = db.query(User).get(id)\\nposts = db.query(Post).filter_by(user_id=id).all()\\n\\n# good - one query through a join\\ndb.query(User).join(Post).filter(User.id == id).all()"],[3335,"The passing of numbers as strings can cause errors in the parsing and lead to redundant conversions of the types. The client can expect a number and receive a string, which will lead to a crash or to wrong logic. That makes the validation harder and makes the contract ambiguous. The risk of a loss of performance because of the constant conversions also grows.","The definition: a JSON contract is the agreement between the server and the client about the structure and the types of the data.\\nWhen the numeric values in a contract are passed as strings, a number of potential problems appear, which it matters to understand when designing an API.\\n1. The typing and the errors of parsing\\n\\n- If the client expects an `Int` field but receives `\\"123\\"`, it has to convert the string manually.\\n\\n- Errors are possible with any mismatch, `\\"12a\\"` instead of `\\"12\\"`, for example.\\n\\n2. The ambiguity of the contract\\n\\n- The server can return `\\"00123\\"`, and the client will transform it into `123`, losing the format.\\n\\n- Different clients can interpret the string differently.\\n\\n3. Losses in the performance\\n\\n- Frequent conversions `\\"123\\".toInt()` create redundant operations.\\n\\n- With a large volume of data that affects the speed noticeably.\\n\\n4. Problems with comparison\\n\\n- Strings are compared by the rules of lexicography, and numbers by their magnitude.\\n\\n- Consequently `\\"20\\"` > `\\"3\\"`, which leads to logical errors.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe use of strings instead of numeric types is permissible only when there is a business reason (leading zeros, for example). In every other case numbers always have to be given as numbers.","val raw = json[\\"count\\"] as String   // \\"15\\"\\nval count = raw.toIntOrNull() ?: 0 // Additional handling"],[3336,"If an array contains objects of different formats, it becomes hard for the client to handle them correctly. The parser cannot know in advance which type of data to expect, which makes the business logic harder. That increases the number of errors, makes the testing harder and breaks the principle of the contract\'s stability. As a result the code becomes less reliable and harder to maintain.","The passing of heterogeneous data structures in one array makes an API unpredictable and the client code complicated. In most cases such solutions break the generally accepted rules of API design.\\n1. The absence of a guaranteed structure\\n\\n- The client does not know which fields a particular element will have.\\n\\n- The need arises for the manual checking of the types and of the presence of the fields.\\n\\n2. The complication of the implementation\\nAn example: the array can contain `\\"user\\"` and `\\"product\\"`, but the client is obliged to determine the type of every element:\\n\\nThat leads to a multitude of branches and to fragile code.\\n3. Problems with the serialization/deserialization\\n\\n- Libraries like Moshi or Gson require additional adapters.\\n\\n- The errors of serialization become harder to detect.\\n\\n4. The difficulties of testing and of documenting\\n\\n- In the contract a multitude of variants of the format appears, which are hard to describe and to check.\\n\\n- The absence of a strict schema increases the risk of a misunderstanding between the teams.\\n\\nConclusion\\nHomogeneous structures in arrays make an API predictable and convenient. Heterogeneous structures are worth using only when there is a clear, formal mechanism for distinguishing the types (a `type` field, for example).","when {\\n    item.has(\\"userId\\") -> parseUser(item)\\n    item.has(\\"productId\\") -> parseProduct(item)\\n}"],[3337,"Unix time is simply a number, which is easy to parse and does not depend on the time zones. Unlike string dates, it requires no complex formats and reduces the risk of errors of conversion. Such a format is easy to compare, to sort and to handle. It ensures uniformity in the interaction of different platforms.","The definition: unix time is the number of seconds or milliseconds that have passed since 1 January 1970 UTC.\\nThe use of string dates leads to an ambiguity of the formats: `\\"2025-01-10\\"`, `\\"10/01/25\\"`, `\\"2025-01-10T14:00Z\\"` - all of those are different variants, which have to be parsed correctly by the client. Unix time solves most of such problems.\\n1. The unambiguity of the format\\n\\n- The format is always numeric (`Long`).\\n\\n- There are no variants of the notation, there is no localization.\\n\\n2. The convenience of the handling\\n\\n- Numbers are easy to compare (`time1 > time2`).\\n\\n- There are fewer errors in the computation of the difference between the dates.\\n\\n3. Universality\\n\\n- Unix time works identically on Android, iOS and the backend.\\n\\n- It does not depend on the time zone or on the locale.\\n\\nAn example of the transformation\\n\\n4. Performance\\n\\n- There is no need to parse a string.\\n\\n- Less memory when passing it.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnix time is convenient in integrations and in the passing of dates in an API. String dates are worth using only if a human-readable format matters.","val date = Date(unixMillis) // The fast creation of a date"],[3338,"When designing backend systems it matters to take the scalability, the reliability and the performance into account. The work with the data, with the failures and with the growth of the load has to be thought through in advance. An important role is played by the services\' boundaries and by the ways in which they interact. The security and the convenience of maintaining the system also have to be taken into account. A good system design is a balance between simplicity and the future requirements.","System design is not about particular technologies, but about taking the right architectural decisions.\\nThe definition\\nSystem design is the process of designing the structure of a backend system taking the load, the failures, the scaling and the maintenance into account.\\n1. Scalability\\nThe system has to withstand the growth of the users and of the data:\\n\\n- horizontal scaling;\\n\\n- stateless services;\\n\\n- the division of the load.\\n\\nAn example - several instances of a service behind a balancer.\\n2. Reliability and fault tolerance\\nIt matters to take into account that failures are the norm:\\n\\n- retries;\\n\\n- timeouts;\\n\\n- fallback logic.\\n\\nIf the database is unavailable, the service has to react correctly.\\n3. The work with the data\\nAt that stage what is decided is:\\n\\n- which database is used;\\n\\n- where a cache is needed;\\n\\n- where eventual consistency is permissible.\\n\\n4. The boundaries of responsibility\\nThe services have to be:\\n\\n- with a clear zone of responsibility;\\n\\n- minimally coupled to one another.\\n\\nThat makes the maintenance and the development easier.\\n5. The operation and the maintenance\\nIt matters to think in advance about:\\n\\n- logging;\\n\\n- metrics;\\n\\n- alerts.\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nThe key to a good system design is to take into account not only the current requirements, but also the future growth, the failures and the maintenance of the system in production.",null],[3339,"Fault tolerance begins with the acceptance of the fact that failures are inevitable. The system has to work correctly when individual components crash. For that, replication, timeouts and retries are used. It matters to avoid a single point of failure. It is also necessary to observe the system constantly and to react to the problems quickly.","The design of fault-tolerant systems requires systematic thinking and an understanding of the real scenarios of failure.\\nThe definition\\nFault tolerance is a system\'s ability to carry on working when its components fail partially.\\n1. The acceptance of the inevitability of failures\\nThe first step is to consider that:\\n\\n- the services can crash;\\n\\n- the network can be unstable;\\n\\n- the responses can arrive with a delay.\\n\\n2. The removal of the single point of failure\\nIt matters to avoid the components on which the whole system depends:\\n\\n- several instances of a service;\\n\\n- the replication of the databases;\\n\\n- reserve nodes.\\n\\n3. Timeouts and retries\\nEvery network call has to have a timeout:\\n\\nThe retries have to be:\\n\\n- limited;\\n\\n- with a delay;\\n\\n- without infinite repetitions.\\n\\n4. Degradation instead of a crash\\nIf part of the system is unavailable:\\n\\n- return a partial result;\\n\\n- switch the non-essential functionality off.\\n\\n5. Observability\\nFor a quick reaction what is needed is:\\n\\n- logs;\\n\\n- metrics;\\n\\n- alerts.\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nA fault-tolerant system does not try to avoid failures but is ready for them. The earlier that is laid into the design, the more stable the system is in production.","requests.get(url, timeout=2)"],[3340,"First you have to understand where exactly the bottleneck arises: the frontend, the backend, the database or the network. Then caching, the limiting of the requests and horizontal scaling are applied. Often the degradation of the functionality and asynchronous handling help. The main thing is to stabilize the system rather than to optimize everything at once.","When the load grows sharply it matters to act in stages and not to make the situation worse.\\n1) The quick measures for stabilization\\nFirst the task of the system\'s survival is solved.\\n1.1) The limiting of the load\\n\\n- a rate limit on the API\\n\\n- the limiting of the heavy endpoints\\n\\n- the temporary switching off of the secondary functions\\n\\n1.2) Caching\\n\\n- an HTTP cache (a CDN)\\n\\n- a server cache (Redis)\\n\\n- the caching of the API\'s responses\\n\\n2) The unburdening of the backend\\nOnce it is stabilized, you move on to the reduction of the load.\\n2.1) Asynchronous handling\\n\\n- queues\\n\\n- background tasks\\n\\n- deferred computations\\n\\nExamples:\\n\\n- the sending of letters\\n\\n- the generation of reports\\n\\n- the integrations\\n\\n2.2) Scaling\\n\\n- the horizontal scaling of the API\\n\\n- a load balancer\\n\\n- autoscaling\\n\\n3) The degradation of the functionality\\nIt matters to be able to \\"break beautifully\\":\\n\\n- switch the complex filters off temporarily\\n\\n- show simplified responses\\n\\n- reduce the precision of the data\\n\\n4) The typical mistake\\n\\n- beginning the optimization of the code without an understanding of the bottleneck\\n\\n- ignoring the database and the network\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nDuring a burst of load, the system is first limited and stabilized, then the backend is unburdened, and only after that is the code and the architecture optimized.","100 req/sec → 429 Too Many Requests\\n\\n// the pseudo-logic\\nif ($cache->has($key)) {\\n    return $cache->get($key);\\n}"],[3341,"Client-server interaction is the model in which the game on the player\'s device (the client) exchanges data with a remote server. The client sends requests, the player\'s actions for example, and the server handles them and returns the result. The server is normally responsible for saving the progress, for checking the rules and for synchronizing the players. Such an approach improves the security and lets the game be scaled. In mobile games HTTP or WebSocket is most often used.","The client-server model is used in almost every modern mobile game - from the casual ones to MMOs. It lets the game logic that has to be protected be separated from the logic that runs on the player\'s device.\\nThe definition\\nClient-server interaction is the architectural approach in which the client application sends requests to the server, and the server handles them and returns the responses.\\nThe main roles\\nBefore moving on to the implementation, it matters to understand who is responsible for what:\\n\\n- The client\\n\\n- The display of the graphics and of the interface\\n\\n- The gathering of the player\'s actions\\n\\n- The sending of the requests to the server\\n\\n- The server\\n\\n- The storage of the player\'s data\\n\\n- The checking of the game\'s rules\\n\\n- The protection against cheats\\n\\n- The synchronization between the players\\n\\nThe ways of interacting\\nIn practice several main approaches are used:\\n\\n- HTTP / REST\\n\\n- It suits turn-based and asynchronous games\\n\\n- It is simple to debug and to scale\\n\\n- WebSocket\\n\\n- It suits real-time interaction\\n\\n- Fewer delays, a persistent connection\\n\\n- Custom TCP/UDP\\n\\n- It is used in complex multiplayer games\\n\\n- It requires more effort in the development\\n\\nAn example of a request from the client (Unity)\\n\\nThe typical mistakes\\n\\n- Holding the critical logic only on the client\\n\\n- The absence of timeouts and of repeated requests\\n\\n- The absence of versioning of the API\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nClient-server interaction is needed when the security, the preservation of the data and the synchronization matter to a game. For most mobile games HTTP is enough, and real-time projects require a persistent connection.","UnityWebRequest request =\\n    UnityWebRequest.Post(url, jsonData);\\nawait request.SendWebRequest();\\n\\n// the handling of the server\'s response"],[3342,"SQL and NoSQL are the two main types of database. SQL databases use a strict structure and tables, and NoSQL more flexible formats of storage. For games with a clear schema of data, SQL is well suited. For quickly changing or scalable data, NoSQL is more often chosen. The choice depends on the load, on the structure of the data and on the requirements for the scaling.","In mobile games a database is used for storing the players\' progress, their purchases, their settings and the statistics. The choice of the type of database directly affects the performance and the convenience of the development.\\nThe definition\\nA database is a system for storing and managing structured data, ensuring fast access and reliability.\\nSQL databases\\nSQL databases suit the cases where the structure of the data is known in advance:\\n\\n- A rigid schema of the data\\n\\n- Tables, rows and relationships\\n\\n- Support for transactions\\n\\nThe typical game scenarios:\\n\\n- The players\' accounts\\n\\n- The payments and the purchases\\n\\n- An inventory with a fixed structure\\n\\nNoSQL databases\\nNoSQL databases are oriented towards flexibility and scaling:\\n\\n- The absence of a strict schema\\n\\n- Storage in the form of documents or key-value\\n\\n- A high speed of writing\\n\\nThe frequent scenarios of use:\\n\\n- The players\' sessions\\n\\n- Temporary data\\n\\n- Telemetry and analytics\\n\\nHow to choose a database\\nWhen choosing, it is worth taking into account:\\n\\n- The structure of the data\\n\\n- Clear and stable → SQL\\n\\n- Flexible and changing → NoSQL\\n\\n- The load\\n\\n- A lot of reading and complex queries → SQL\\n\\n- A lot of writing and scaling → NoSQL\\n\\n- The size of the team\\n\\n- A small team → simple solutions\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nSQL suits stable, critical data, and NoSQL flexible, heavily loaded scenarios. In real projects a combination of both approaches is often used.",null],[3343,"Processes on different devices cannot be synchronized through the ordinary local primitives. For that the network and external coordinating systems are used. Often databases, message queues or specialized services are applied. They let the processes exchange state and signals. The choice of approach depends on the requirements for the reliability and the delays.","When processes work on different machines, they have no shared memory and no shared locks.\\nWhy the local mechanisms do not suit\\nMechanisms like:\\n\\n- `Lock`\\n\\n- `Semaphore`\\n\\n- `Condition`\\n\\nwork only inside one process or OS. Between devices they are useless.\\nThe main approaches to the synchronization\\nFor distributed synchronization, external points of agreement are used:\\n1. Through a storage of the state\\nThe processes read and change the shared state:\\n\\n- a database\\n\\n- a key-value storage\\n\\nAn example of the logic:\\n\\n- process A has written the status\\n\\n- process B has checked it and carried on working\\n\\n2. Through message queues\\nThe processes exchange events:\\n\\n- \\"the task is ready\\"\\n\\n- \\"the operation has completed\\"\\n\\nThe queue guarantees the delivery and the order (within the model\'s limits).\\n3. Through distributed coordinators\\nSpecialized systems are used:\\n\\n- for locks\\n\\n- for leader election\\n\\n- for signals\\n\\nThey solve the problems of races and of network failures.\\nThe problems that have to be taken into account\\n\\n- the network delays\\n\\n- the loss of the connection\\n\\n- the partial crash of nodes\\n\\nSynchronization in distributed systems is always less reliable than local synchronization.\\nA short conclusion\\nFor processes on different devices, the synchronization is always built through the network and through external services. The higher the requirements for the reliability, the more complex the solution.",null],[3344,"S3 is used for storing files and objects: images, videos, logs, backups and static content. That storage is not about \\"queries\\" but about the reliable storage and serving of objects by a key. S3 scales well and suits large volumes of data. It is normally used as an external storage rather than as a database.","S3 is not a file system and not a database, but an object storage with a simple, reliable interface.\\nThe definition\\nS3 (object storage) is an object storage in which the data is saved as objects under a key and is available through an API.\\nThe typical scenarios of use\\n\\n- The storage of users\' files\\n\\n- Avatars\\n\\n- Documents\\n\\n- Videos and images\\n\\n- Static content\\n\\n- Files for a CDN\\n\\n- Frontend builds\\n\\n- Logs and dumps\\n\\n- Archives of logs\\n\\n- The results of the export of data\\n\\n- Backups\\n\\n- The reserve copies of a database\\n\\n- Snapshots of the data\\n\\n- A data lake\\n\\n- Raw events\\n\\n- Historical data for analytics\\n\\nWhy S3 suits those tasks\\n\\n- Scalability\\n\\n- An almost unlimited volume.\\n\\n- Reliability\\n\\n- Replication and high availability.\\n\\n- The simplicity of the access\\n\\n- `GET` / `PUT` by the object\'s key.\\n\\n- Cheap storage\\n\\n- Compared with disks and databases.\\n\\nWhat should not be done with S3\\n\\n- Using it as a database.\\n\\n- Storing data that requires frequent updates \\"inside the file\\".\\n\\n- Expecting low delays like an in-memory solution\'s.\\n\\nConclusion\\nS3 is ideally suited to storing files, archives and large volumes of data, but it does not replace a database or a cache.",null],[3345,"A REST API is well suited to the classic client-server applications where there are CRUD operations on resources. It is simple, comprehensible and easily debugged over HTTP. REST is convenient to use for public APIs and for integrations. Most often it is chosen for its predictability and for its wide support by the tools.","REST is an architectural style built around resources and around HTTP\'s standard capabilities.\\nThe definition\\nREST (Representational State Transfer) is an approach to building an API in which the operations are performed on resources through the standard HTTP methods.\\nWhere REST suits best of all\\n\\n- CRUD services\\n\\n- Users\\n\\n- Orders\\n\\n- Products\\n\\n- Public APIs\\n\\n- The integrations with external systems.\\n\\n- An API for partners.\\n\\n- Simple client-server applications\\n\\n- Web\\n\\n- Mobile\\n\\n- Systems with caching\\n\\n- It works well with the HTTP cache.\\n\\n- Support for `ETag`, `If-Modified-Since`.\\n\\nWhy REST is often chosen\\n\\n- Simplicity\\n\\n- A comprehensible model of resources.\\n\\n- HTTP\'s standards\\n\\n- `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`.\\n\\n- The tools and the ecosystem\\n\\n- The logs, the proxies and the monitoring work \\"out of the box\\".\\n\\nREST\'s limitations\\n\\n- Redundant data (overfetching).\\n\\n- A lot of requests for complex screens.\\n\\n- Weak typing compared with contract APIs.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA REST API is better used for the standard CRUD scenarios, for public interfaces and for simple services, where comprehensibility and compatibility matter.",null],[3346,"GraphQL is better used when the clients need different sets of data and it matters to avoid redundant requests and redundant fields. The client itself describes which data it needs and receives exactly that. That is especially convenient for complex interfaces and for mobile applications. GraphQL does, however, require a more complex server implementation and a strict control of the performance.","GraphQL solves the problem of \\"one endpoint - many variants of data\\", shifting the forming of the response onto the client.\\nThe definition\\nGraphQL is a query language for an API and an environment for running those queries, in which the client states explicitly which data it needs.\\nWhen GraphQL suits best of all\\n\\n- Complex client interfaces\\n\\n- One screen requires data from different domains.\\n\\n- The number of HTTP requests has to be reduced.\\n\\n- Different clients with different needs\\n\\n- Web, mobile, an admin panel.\\n\\n- Every client requests its own set of fields.\\n\\n- Quickly changing requirements for the data\\n\\n- Frequent changes of the UI without changes to the server endpoints.\\n\\nThe advantages\\n\\n- There is no overfetching or underfetching\\n\\n- The client receives only the fields it needs.\\n\\n- One endpoint\\n\\n- It makes the routing and the versioning easier.\\n\\n- An explicit schema\\n\\n- The contract between the client and the server.\\n\\nThe limitations and the risks\\n\\n- It is harder to cache at the level of HTTP.\\n\\n- The risk of expensive queries without limits.\\n\\n- A higher complexity of the server and of the maintenance.\\n\\nConclusion\\nGraphQL is worth using for complex client applications with different requirements for the data, if the team is ready for the additional complexity.",null],[3347,"gRPC is better used for the interaction between services inside a system. It is fast, strictly typed and works over HTTP/2. The contracts are described in advance, which reduces the number of errors. gRPC is poorly suited to public APIs and to browsers without an additional layer.","gRPC is oriented towards the efficient, reliable interaction of services rather than towards the universality of an HTTP API.\\nThe definition\\ngRPC is an RPC framework using Protocol Buffers and HTTP/2 for the fast, type-safe exchange of data.\\nWhere gRPC is especially good\\n\\n- Internal microservices\\n\\n- Frequent calls.\\n\\n- Low delays.\\n\\n- Contract APIs\\n\\n- Strict schemas.\\n\\n- The autogeneration of the clients.\\n\\n- Stream data\\n\\n- Server streaming.\\n\\n- Bidirectional streaming.\\n\\nThe advantages\\n\\n- High performance\\n\\n- A binary format.\\n\\n- HTTP/2.\\n\\n- Type safety\\n\\n- The contract is obligatory.\\n\\n- The generation of code\\n\\n- Fewer manual errors.\\n\\nThe limitations\\n\\n- It is poorly suited to browsers.\\n\\n- It is harder to debug manually.\\n\\n- It requires the maintenance of protobuf contracts.\\n\\nConclusion\\ngRPC is an excellent choice for the internal inter-service interaction, where the speed, the typing and stable contracts matter.",null],[3348,"All of the data inside a JWT is visible to the client. A JWT is encoded in Base64 rather than encrypted. Anyone who has the token can look at its content. Secret data must not therefore be put in a JWT.","A JWT is often mistakenly perceived as a \\"safe container\\", although in fact it is transparent to the client.\\nThe definition\\nA JWT (JSON Web Token) is a token consisting of three parts (the header, the payload, the signature), in which the payload is available for anyone to read.\\nWhat exactly is visible to the client\\n\\n- The header\\n\\n- The type of token.\\n\\n- The signature algorithm.\\n\\n- The payload\\n\\n- The user data (the claims).\\n\\n- The standard fields (`sub`, `exp`, `iat`).\\n\\n- The token\'s structure\\n\\n- The parts are separated by full stops.\\n\\nAn example of a payload:\\n\\nWhat a JWT does NOT protect\\n\\n- The confidentiality of the data.\\n\\n- The concealment of the authorization logic.\\n\\nThe signature guarantees the integrity, but does not hide the content.\\nThe practical rule\\n\\n- In a JWT what is put is only:\\n\\n- identifiers,\\n\\n- roles,\\n\\n- service flags.\\n\\n- What is never put in:\\n\\n- passwords,\\n\\n- the tokens of third-party services,\\n\\n- personal secrets.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA JWT can be read from any side, so it suits the passing of assertions about a user, but not the storage of secret data.","{\\n  \\"sub\\": \\"user_123\\",\\n  \\"role\\": \\"admin\\",\\n  \\"exp\\": 1700000000\\n}"],[3349,"If you change any data in a JWT, its signature will become invalid. The server will detect that during the check and will reject the token. Even the change of one character in the payload will lead to a validation error. A JWT cannot therefore be \\"touched up\\" on the client unnoticed.","A JWT protects the data not by encryption but by a cryptographic signature, which guarantees its integrity.\\nThe definition\\nA JWT\'s signature is a cryptographic check that confirms that the header and the payload have not been changed since the server issued the token.\\nHow the check works\\n\\n- The server creates the token\\n\\n- It forms the `header` and the `payload`.\\n\\n- It signs them with a secret key or with a private key.\\n\\n- The client holds and passes the token\\n\\n- Normally without changes.\\n\\n- The server checks the signature\\n\\n- It recomputes the signature.\\n\\n- It compares it with the one that came in the token.\\n\\nWhat happens when the data is changed\\n\\n- The payload changes → the hash changes.\\n\\n- The signature no longer matches.\\n\\n- The server gets a check error.\\n\\n- The request is considered unauthorized.\\n\\nA frequent myth\\n\\n- \\"A JWT can be touched up, since it is Base64\\" - that is wrong.\\nBase64 is an encoding, and the protection is built on the signature.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAny change of the data in a JWT makes it invalid, so the token\'s integrity is reliably protected by the signature.",null],[3350,"Most often a JWT is passed in the `Authorization` HTTP header. Normally the `Bearer` scheme is used. The client sends the token with every request to the protected endpoints. The server extracts the token and checks its validity.","A JWT is a stateless mechanism, so the token has to be passed in every request where authorization is needed.\\nThe definition\\nA bearer token is a way of passing a token in which the very fact of possessing the token is considered proof of the authorization.\\nThe main ways of passing a JWT\\n1) The Authorization HTTP header (recommended)\\n\\n- The format:\\n\\n- The universal, standard approach.\\n\\n- It works well with proxies, with logging and with middleware.\\n\\n2) A cookie\\n\\n- It is often used in browser applications.\\n\\n- It can be `HttpOnly` and `Secure`.\\n\\n- CSRF has to be taken into account.\\n\\n3) Query parameters (not recommended)\\n\\n- The token can get into the logs.\\n\\n- It is vulnerable to leaks through the referrer.\\n\\nWhat matters to take into account\\n\\n- The stateless approach\\n\\n- The server holds no sessions.\\n\\n- All of the information is in the token.\\n\\n- The lifetime\\n\\n- A JWT is normally short-lived.\\n\\n- Refresh tokens are used.\\n\\n- The security of the passing\\n\\n- Always over HTTPS.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe standard and safest way of passing a JWT is through the `Authorization: Bearer` header; the other variants are used only under particular requirements.","Authorization: Bearer <jwt_token>"],[3351,"A JWT\'s main problem is the leak of the token: anyone who has it can use it until its term expires. A JWT is hard to revoke before the `exp` unless additional mechanisms are used. With a wrong configuration, vulnerabilities related to the signature algorithms can arise. There are also the risks of XSS and of the wrong storage of the token on the client.","A JWT is a tool that requires careful use and a clear understanding of its limitations.\\nThe definition\\nA JWT\'s vulnerabilities are the risks related to the storage, the passing and the validation of the tokens rather than to the format itself.\\nThe main security problems\\n1) The leak of a token\\n\\n- A token can be stolen through XSS.\\n\\n- Anyone who has the token is considered authorized.\\n\\n2) The difficulty of revocation\\n\\n- The stateless nature.\\n\\n- Without blacklists a token lives until its `exp`.\\n\\n3) A long lifetime\\n\\n- The larger the `exp`, the higher the risk.\\n\\n- It is often solved with refresh tokens.\\n\\n4) Errors of validation\\n\\n- An unchecked signature algorithm.\\n\\n- The ignoring of the `exp`, `aud`, `iss`.\\n\\n5) The storage on the client\\n\\n- LocalStorage is vulnerable to XSS.\\n\\n- Cookies require protection against CSRF.\\n\\nThe practical measures of protection\\n\\n- A short TTL for the access tokens.\\n\\n- Refresh tokens with server-side storage.\\n\\n- The checking of every claim.\\n\\n- The use of HTTPS.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA JWT is safe only with the right configuration: a short lifetime for the token, strict validation and protection against leaks are obligatory.",null],[3352,"The main problems are related to the memory, the performance and the time of the handling. Large responses can block the main thread. Difficulties arise with the parsing, the storage and the display of the data. It also matters to take the network errors and the partial loading into account. Without optimizations the application becomes slow and unstable.","Work with a large volume of data is a frequent source of problems in mobile applications.\\nThe main categories of difficulty\\nBefore the analysis it matters to understand that the problem is rarely only in the network - more often it is a complex one.\\n1. The consumption of memory\\nLarge JSON responses can:\\n\\n- take up a lot of main memory;\\n\\n- lead to a memory warning;\\n\\n- cause crashes.\\n\\nThe practices for reducing the risk:\\n\\n- loading by pages;\\n\\n- the streaming of the data;\\n\\n- the refusal to hold the whole response in memory.\\n\\n2. The performance of the parsing\\nThe parsing of a large JSON:\\n\\n- loads the CPU;\\n\\n- can block the UI;\\n\\n- increases the time to the first display.\\n\\nThe solutions:\\n\\n- perform the parsing in a background queue;\\n\\n- use lighter DTOs;\\n\\n- decode only the fields needed.\\n\\n3. The display in the UI\\nThe problems arise when:\\n\\n- the tables contain thousands of elements;\\n\\n- the data is updated entirely;\\n\\n- there are no diffs.\\n\\nThe approaches:\\n\\n- batch updates;\\n\\n- a diffable data source;\\n\\n- lazy computations.\\n\\n4. The network risks\\nLarge responses:\\n\\n- take longer to load;\\n\\n- are interrupted more often;\\n\\n- are harder to repeat.\\n\\nIt matters:\\n\\n- to be able to load the data in further portions;\\n\\n- to handle the errors correctly;\\n\\n- to support retry.\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\nWhen working with a large volume of data it matters to limit the loading, to handle the data outside the main thread and to update the UI incrementally. That directly affects the stability and the user experience.",null],[3353,"You have to model the states of loading, of success and of error explicitly. The user has to understand what is happening. The errors should be handled centrally and comprehensible messages should be shown. It matters to support the repetition of a request and its cancellation. The UI must not \\"hang\\" without feedback.","Long network operations require careful work with the states; otherwise the user loses trust in the application.\\nThe modelling of the states\\nBefore the implementation it matters to determine all of the screen\'s possible states.\\nThe typical set:\\n\\n- `idle` - the operation has not begun yet.\\n\\n- `loading` - the loading is in progress.\\n\\n- `success` - the data has been obtained.\\n\\n- `error` - an error has happened.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe display of the states in the UI\\nFor every state there has to be a comprehensible visual response:\\n\\n- an indicator of the loading;\\n\\n- a placeholder with the error;\\n\\n- the content;\\n\\n- a repeat button.\\n\\nThe UI should not depend on the error\'s details.\\nThe handling of the errors\\nThe errors are better:\\n\\n- normalized into a domain model;\\n\\n- not shown to the user as a raw error;\\n\\n- logged separately.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe repetition and the cancellation\\nLong operations must obligatorily:\\n\\n- support retry;\\n\\n- be able to be cancelled when the screen is left;\\n\\n- complete correctly when the state changes.\\n\\nAsynchrony and the threads\\nIt matters:\\n\\n- not to update the UI from a background thread;\\n\\n- not to allow race conditions;\\n\\n- to handle the result only if the screen is active.\\n\\nThe practical conclusion\\nThe explicit management of the states of loading and of errors is the key to a stable UX during long network operations. The user always has to understand what is happening and what they can do next.","enum LoadState {\\n    case idle\\n    case loading\\n    case success(Data)\\n    case error(Error)\\n}\\n\\nfunc mapError(_ error: Error) -> UserFacingError"],[3354,"You have to start the request so that it is tied to the screen\'s lifecycle and can be cancelled. The UI is updated only on the main thread. The errors and the states of loading have to be handled explicitly. It matters to avoid a retain cycle and the updating of the UI after the screen has been closed. It is better to keep the network logic outside the controller and to leave the controller only the display of the state.","Asynchronous requests in a controller are dangerous in that the network lives \\"longer\\" than the screen. The right approach is therefore to tie the request to the lifecycle and to keep the management of the state transparent.\\n1) The basic requirements for a correct implementation\\nBefore the code it matters to fix the rules that always have to be observed.\\n\\n- The UI is updated only on the main thread.\\n\\n- The request has to be cancellable.\\n\\n- The request\'s result should not be applied if the screen is no longer current.\\n\\n- There should be no memory leaks because of `self` in the closures or the tasks.\\n\\n- The `loading/success/error` states have to be explicit.\\n\\n2) The tying to the lifecycle and the cancellation\\nThe variant with async/await and a Task\\nNormally a reference to the task is held and it is cancelled when the screen is left.\\n\\nThe key details:\\n\\n- `loadTask` is held so that it can be cancelled.\\n\\n- `Task { [weak self] ... }` reduces the risk of a retain cycle.\\n\\n- The UI is updated inside `MainActor.run`.\\n\\nWhy the cancellation matters\\nIf you do not cancel:\\n\\n- the request will carry on;\\n\\n- the result can arrive after the `pop/dismiss`;\\n\\n- redundant updates, leaks and races of the state are possible.\\n\\n3) The protection against races and against repeated requests\\nA frequent problem: the user tugs at the `pull-to-refresh` while the old request is still in progress.\\nThe practices:\\n\\n- cancel the previous task before starting a new one;\\n\\n- hold the current state of the loading;\\n\\n- ignore the out-of-date responses (by a `requestId`, for example).\\n\\n4) The explicit management of the screen\'s states\\nThe controller has to be able to \\"draw\\" the state in one way rather than piecemeal.\\nThe approach:\\n\\n- introduce a `ScreenState`;\\n\\n- update it centrally;\\n\\n- have one `render(state:)` method.\\n\\nEven without a full MVVM that reduces the chaos in the controller sharply.\\n5) Where the boundary of responsibility runs\\nThe right separation normally looks like this:\\n\\n- The controller:\\n\\n- starts the loading;\\n\\n- displays the states;\\n\\n- handles the user\'s actions.\\n\\n- The service/repository:\\n\\n- performs the requests;\\n\\n- decodes the data;\\n\\n- returns the models or the domain errors.\\n\\nIf the decoding and the building of the models happen inside the controller, that is almost always a sign of a future Massive VC.\\nThe practical conclusion\\nThe correct handling of asynchronous requests in a view controller is built on three things: the cancellation and the tie to the lifecycle, the updating of the UI only on the main thread, and the explicit management of the states (loading/success/error). The more of the network and mapping logic is moved out of the controller, the more stable and maintainable the screen is.","final class FeedViewController: UIViewController {\\n    private var loadTask: Task<Void, Never>?\\n\\n    override func viewDidLoad() {\\n        super.viewDidLoad()\\n        load()\\n    }\\n\\n    override func viewDidDisappear(_ animated: Bool) {\\n        super.viewDidDisappear(animated)\\n        loadTask?.cancel()\\n    }\\n\\n    private func load() {\\n        setLoading(true)\\n\\n        loadTask = Task { [weak self] in\\n            guard let self else { return }\\n\\n            do {\\n                let items = try await self.service.fetchFeed() // the service hides URLSession\\n                await MainActor.run {\\n                    self.setLoading(false)\\n                    self.render(items)\\n                }\\n            } catch {\\n                await MainActor.run {\\n                    self.setLoading(false)\\n                    self.showError(error)\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nloadTask?.cancel()\\nloadTask = Task { ... }"],[3355,"For parallel requests `DispatchGroup`, `async/await` or `OperationQueue` are used. These tools let you start several tasks at once and receive a notification when all of them have completed. The choice of approach depends on the iOS version and on the project\'s architecture. Modern Swift prefers `async/await`.","The running of several network requests is a typical task for client applications.\\nDispatchGroup\\nIt suits GCD-oriented code:\\n\\nasync/await\\nThe definition: `async/await` is the modern way of working with asynchronous code.\\n\\nOperationQueue\\nIt is used when:\\n\\n- dependencies are needed\\n\\n- cancellation is required\\n\\n- the control of the operations\' state matters\\n\\nA short conclusion\\nFor new projects it is better to use `async/await`. `DispatchGroup` remains current for GCD code and for the old iOS versions.","let group = DispatchGroup()\\n\\ngroup.enter()\\nrequestA { group.leave() }\\n\\ngroup.enter()\\nrequestB { group.leave() }\\n\\ngroup.notify(queue: .main) {\\n    // all of the requests have completed\\n}\\n\\nasync let a = loadA()\\nasync let b = loadB()\\n\\nlet resultA = await a\\nlet resultB = await b"],[3356,"The number of simultaneous requests can be limited by means of an `OperationQueue`, of semaphores or of the configuration of `URLSession`. That lets you control the load and avoid problems with the resources. The safest way is an `OperationQueue` with a set `maxConcurrentOperationCount`.","The limiting of the parallelism matters for the application\'s stable work.\\nOperationQueue\\nThe most readable, safest way:\\n\\nEvery request is made into an `Operation`.\\nDispatchSemaphore\\nA low-level but workable variant:\\n\\nIt requires careful use, in order to avoid a deadlock.\\nURLSession\\n`URLSessionConfiguration` has:\\n\\n- internal limits\\n\\n- the management of the connections per host\\n\\nIt suits a basic control.\\nA short conclusion\\nAn `OperationQueue` is the preferable tool for limiting the number of parallel network requests. Semaphores are worth using only when necessary.","let queue = OperationQueue()\\nqueue.maxConcurrentOperationCount = 4\\n\\nlet semaphore = DispatchSemaphore(value: 2)\\n\\nsemaphore.wait()\\n// the network request\\nsemaphore.signal()"],[3357,"The main way of working with the network in iOS is `URLSession`. Third-party libraries such as Alamofire are also used. For WebSocket connections specialized solutions are applied. In modern projects Swift Concurrency is actively used. The choice of approach depends on the project\'s complexity and requirements.","iOS provides several levels of abstraction for working with the network.\\n`URLSession`\\nThis is the basic tool for network requests.\\n\\n- It supports HTTP and HTTPS\\n\\n- It works with background and foreground tasks\\n\\n- It lets you manage the timeouts and the cache\\n\\nIt suits most network tasks.\\nThird-party libraries\\nThe popular libraries make the work with the network easier.\\n\\n- They reduce the amount of boilerplate code\\n\\n- They provide convenient APIs\\n\\n- They often include the handling of the errors and logging\\n\\nThey are used over `URLSession`.\\nWebSocket and streaming\\nFor the real-time scenarios what is applied is:\\n\\n- WebSocket clients\\n\\n- Long-lived connections\\n\\n- The stream passing of data\\n\\nThat is a separate class of network task.\\nThe modern approaches\\nWith the appearance of Swift Concurrency:\\n\\n- `async/await` is used\\n\\n- The code becomes linear and readable\\n\\n- The handling of the errors becomes easier\\n\\nConclusion\\n`URLSession` is the foundation of iOS\'s network stack. The other tools either make its use easier or solve specialized tasks.",null],[3358,"`URLSession` is a low-level system API for working with the network. Alamofire and Moya are wrapper libraries over it. They reduce the volume of boilerplate code and make the configuration of the requests easier. `URLSession` does, however, give the maximum control and the minimum of dependencies. The choice depends on the project\'s complexity and on the requirements for the architecture.","The difference between those tools lies not in the network capabilities but in the level of abstraction.\\n`URLSession`\\n`URLSession` is the foundation of iOS\'s network stack.\\n\\n- It provides full control over the requests\\n\\n- It works directly with `URLRequest` and `URLResponse`\\n\\n- It supports background tasks, caching and timeouts\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- The minimum of dependencies\\n\\n- The maximum transparency\\n\\n- It is excellently suited to simple and medium projects\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- A lot of boilerplate code\\n\\n- Repeated logic for handling the errors\\n\\n- The network layer is harder to scale\\n\\nAlamofire\\nAlamofire is a convenient wrapper over `URLSession`.\\n\\n- A more readable API\\n\\n- Built-in handling of the errors\\n\\n- Support for interceptors and retry\\n\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- A quick introduction\\n\\n- Less boilerplate code\\n\\n- It is good for REST APIs\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- An additional dependency\\n\\n- Part of the logic is hidden inside the library\\n\\nMoya\\nMoya adds one more level - the description of the API through an enum.\\n\\n- A clear description of the endpoints\\n\\n- It is convenient for large teams\\n\\n- It combines well with testing\\n\\nThe minuses:\\n\\n- A higher barrier to entry\\n\\n- An even greater level of abstraction\\n\\n- It is not always justified for simple projects\\n\\nConclusion\\n`URLSession` is the better choice when the control and the simplicity matter.\\nAlamofire speeds the development up and reduces the volume of code.\\nMoya is justified in large projects with a large number of endpoints and a complex architecture. The higher the abstraction, the greater the convenience, but the less the transparency.",null],[3359,"Starscream is a library for working with WebSocket in iOS. It is used for the real-time updating of data. It suits chats, live notifications and the streaming of events. It works over the standard network mechanisms. It makes the management of the connection\'s state easier.","Not every network task is solved by ordinary HTTP requests.\\nWhy WebSocket is needed\\nWebSocket is used when what is required is:\\n\\n- A persistent connection\\n\\n- A two-way exchange of data\\n\\n- Minimal delays\\n\\nThe typical examples:\\n\\n- Chats\\n\\n- Online games\\n\\n- The live updating of data\\n\\nStarscream\\nStarscream is a popular library for WebSocket in iOS.\\n\\n- It manages the connection\'s lifecycle\\n\\n- It handles the reconnect\\n\\n- It supports the events of the connection and of the errors\\n\\nIt hides the protocol\'s complexity and provides a convenient API.\\nThe limitations of the use\\n\\n- WebSocket consumes more resources\\n\\n- It requires the control of the background mode\\n\\n- It is not always needed, if polling or push is enough\\n\\nConclusion\\nStarscream solves a narrow but important task - real-time communication. It is worth using only when a persistent connection really is justified; otherwise HTTP remains the simpler, more reliable solution.",null],[3360,"GraphQL is an approach to an API in which the client itself describes the data it needs. Apollo is a client library for working with GraphQL in iOS. It generates the types and the queries automatically. That reduces the number of errors and makes the maintenance easier. It is especially useful in large projects.","GraphQL solves the problems that REST APIs often run into.\\nWhat GraphQL is\\nThe definition:\\nGraphQL is a query language for an API, letting the client request exactly the data it needs.\\nThe advantages:\\n\\n- There is no overfetching\\n\\n- There is no underfetching\\n\\n- One endpoint instead of a multitude\\n\\nApollo\\nApollo is the main client for GraphQL in iOS.\\n\\n- It generates Swift models from the schema\\n\\n- It checks the queries at the stage of the compilation\\n\\n- It supports caching and the updating of the data\\n\\nThat reduces the number of run-time errors considerably.\\nWhen GraphQL is justified\\n\\n- A large number of screens\\n\\n- Complex models of data\\n\\n- The active development of the backend\\n\\nWhen it is not worth using\\n\\n- A simple API\\n\\n- A small application\\n\\n- The absence of support for GraphQL on the backend\\n\\nConclusion\\nGraphQL with Apollo gives a powerful, type-safe network layer, but it increases the project\'s complexity. It is excellently suited to large applications, where the control over the data matters more than the simplicity, but it can be redundant for small solutions.",null],[3361,"An HTTP request consists of a start line, headers and a body. The start line defines the type of the request and the resource\'s address. The headers describe the request\'s parameters and the data about the client. The request\'s body is used for passing data to the server. Some HTTP methods may contain no body.","An HTTP request is a structured message that the client sends to the server in order to perform an operation.\\nThe definition\\nAn HTTP request is a message sent by the client to the server over the HTTP protocol, containing instructions about which action has to be performed on a resource.\\nThe main parts of an HTTP request\\nAny HTTP request logically consists of three main parts:\\n\\n- the start line (the Request Line)\\n\\n- the headers\\n\\n- the request\'s body\\n\\nEvery part performs its own role and is used by the server when handling the request.\\nThe start line (the Request Line)\\nThe start line always comes first and contains the basic information about the request.\\nIt consists of:\\n\\n- the HTTP method\\n\\n- the path to the resource\\n\\n- the protocol\'s version\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhere:\\n\\n- `GET` is the method\\n\\n- `/users/42` is the path\\n\\n- `HTTP/1.1` is the protocol\'s version\\n\\nThe headers\\nAfter the start line come the request\'s headers.\\nThey pass additional information about the request and the client to the server.\\nThe headers are used for:\\n\\n- stating the format of the data\\n\\n- passing the authorization token\\n\\n- managing the caching\\n\\n- describing the client\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe headers contain no business data, only meta-information.\\nThe request\'s body\\nThe request\'s body is used for passing data to the server.\\nIt is not present in every request.\\nThe body is most often used in:\\n\\n- POST\\n\\n- PUT\\n\\n- PATCH\\n\\nAn example of a body:\\n\\nIt matters to understand:\\n\\n- `GET` normally contains no body\\n\\n- the presence of a body depends on the method and on the request\'s purpose\\n\\nWhat can be absent\\nIn an HTTP request:\\n\\n- the body can be absent\\n\\n- the headers can be minimal\\n\\n- the start line is always present\\n\\nA practical example of a whole request\\n\\nWhy that matters for an iOS developer\\nAn understanding of the structure of an HTTP request is necessary for:\\n\\n- working with `URLRequest`\\n\\n- debugging network problems\\n\\n- the correct configuration of the headers\\n\\n- an understanding of the backend\'s errors\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn HTTP request always consists of a start line, headers and, when necessary, a body. A clear understanding of the request\'s structure lets you work with the network confidently, form the requests correctly and find the errors faster when integrating with an API.","GET /users/42 HTTP/1.1\\n\\nContent-Type: application/json\\nAuthorization: Bearer token\\nAccept-Language: en-US\\n\\n{\\n  \\"name\\": \\"John\\",\\n  \\"email\\": \\"john@example.com\\"\\n}\\n\\nPOST /login HTTP/1.1\\nContent-Type: application/json\\nAuthorization: Bearer token\\n\\n{\\n  \\"email\\": \\"user@mail.com\\",\\n  \\"password\\": \\"123456\\"\\n}"],[3362,"In an SSR application the first page is rendered on the server, but once the JavaScript has loaded it takes over (hydration). After that the interactivity and the pagination work on the client without a full reload: the router intercepts the transitions and loads the data through fetch or an API. A full reload is needed only on a direct visit by URL or with JS switched off.","SSR (Server-Side Rendering) means the HTML is formed on the server and arrives at the client ready-made. That speeds the first paint up and improves the SEO. The HTML in itself is static, however: the buttons, the filters and the pagination do not work until the JavaScript has run on the client and the hydration has happened — the binding of the handlers to the DOM already drawn.\\nHow the pagination works after the hydration\\nAfter the hydration the application turns into an ordinary SPA. The client router (the Next.js Router or React Router, for example) intercepts the clicks on the pagination links and, rather than a full reload, performs the transition on the client.\\n\\n- The user clicks \\"Page 2\\".\\n\\n- The router changes the URL through the History API without a reload.\\n\\n- The component requests the new data through `fetch`, or obtains it from the cache.\\n\\n- React repaints only the list rather than the whole page.\\n\\nAn example in Next.js\\n\\nOn a click `router.push` changes the URL and loads the data through `getServerSideProps` on the server, but without a full reload of the page — Next.js does that as a client-side transition. If the user opens `/items/2` directly, the server will deliver finished HTML.\\nWhen a full reload does nevertheless happen\\n\\n- The direct typing of a URL in the address bar, or F5.\\n\\n- JavaScript is switched off or has not loaded.\\n\\n- A transition by an ordinary `<a href>` link, without interception by the router.\\n\\nIn summary: SSR gives a fast first render, while the interactivity and the pagination after the hydration work as in an SPA — without a full reload. Such an approach is worth applying when both the SEO and a responsive interface matter.","// pages/items/[page].js\\nimport { useRouter } from \'next/router\';\\n\\nexport async function getServerSideProps({ params }) {\\n  const res = await fetch(`https://api.example.com/items?page=${params.page}`);\\n  const items = await res.json();\\n  return { props: { items, page: Number(params.page) } };\\n}\\n\\nexport default function Items({ items, page }) {\\n  const router = useRouter();\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {items.map(i => <div key={i.id}>{i.title}</div>)}\\n      <button onClick={() => router.push(`/items/${page + 1}`)}>\\n        Next\\n      </button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[3363,"Yes, a component with \\"use client\\" is rendered on the server on the first request anyway — the directive only marks the entry point into the client bundle. Server components run only on the server, do not reach the JS bundle and cannot use hooks or event handlers. Client components are rendered both on the server (for the HTML) and on the client (for the interactivity).","In React Server Components (RSC) and the Next.js App Router, the components are divided into two types: server ones (by default) and client ones (marked with the `\\"use client\\"` directive). Many people think mistakenly that a client component is not rendered on the server at all, but that is not so.\\nWhat the `\\"use client\\"` directive does\\nThe directive does not mean \\"render only in the browser\\". It marks the boundary between the server and the client code: everything imported from that module reaches the client bundle. On the first request such a component is run on the server anyway, in order to generate the HTML (SSR), and then the hydration happens on the client.\\nThe differences between server and client components\\n\\n- Server ones: they run only on the server; they can address a database, the file system or the secrets directly; they do not increase the JS bundle; they do not support `useState`, `useEffect` or `onClick`.\\n\\n- Client ones: they are rendered on the server (the HTML) and on the client (the hydration); they support hooks, events and the browser APIs; they increase the bundle\'s size.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nA server component can import a client one, but not the other way round (a client component cannot import a server one directly — only through props/children).\\nIn summary: apply server components for the data, the heavy logic and the SEO content, and client ones only where interactivity is needed, in order to minimize the JS bundle and speed the loading up.","// app/page.tsx — a server component (by default)\\nimport Counter from \'./Counter\';\\nimport { db } from \'./db\';\\n\\nexport default async function Page() {\\n  const users = await db.user.findMany(); // direct access to the database\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Users: {users.length}</h1>\\n      <Counter /> {/* the client component */}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// app/Counter.tsx — a client component\\n\'use client\';\\nimport { useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nexport default function Counter() {\\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0);\\n  return <button onClick={() => setN(n + 1)}>{n}</button>;\\n}"],[3364,"A hydration error arises when the HTML rendered on the server does not match what React renders on the client. The frequent causes: `Date.now()`, `Math.random()`, access to `window` or `localStorage` directly in the render, a different locale format. The solution is to move such computations into a `useEffect` or to use a dynamic import with `ssr: false`. `suppressHydrationWarning` also helps for deliberate discrepancies.","Hydration is the process in which React on the client \\"brings to life\\" the HTML already rendered by the server. If the server\'s and the client\'s markup differ by even a single character, React throws the error `Text content does not match server-rendered HTML` and repaints the subtree, which hurts the performance and the SEO.\\nThe main causes\\n\\n- The use of `Date.now()`, `Math.random()` or `new Date()` directly in the JSX.\\n\\n- Access to `window`, `localStorage` or `navigator` during the render.\\n\\n- Different locales or time zones on the server and on the client.\\n\\n- Incorrect HTML inside the tags (a `<p>` inside a `<p>`, for example), which the browser \\"fixes\\" in its own way.\\n\\n- Conditional rendering that depends on data available only on the client.\\n\\nHow to fix it\\nMove the client-side computations into a `useEffect`, so that the first render matches the server\'s and the state is then updated.\\n\\nIf a component should not be rendered on the server at all, use a dynamic import with `ssr: false`.\\nFor deliberate discrepancies (the design theme, for example), apply `suppressHydrationWarning` on the particular element, but not as a universal solution.\\nThe check\\n\\n- Open the DevTools and find the component where the error arises.\\n\\n- Compare the server\'s HTML (View Source) with the client\'s DOM.\\n\\n- Remove from the render everything that depends on the environment.\\n\\nIn summary: apply a `useEffect` and a dynamic import for the client-side data, and `suppressHydrationWarning` only in a targeted way, in order to preserve SSR\'s advantages and to avoid redundant repaints.","function Clock() {\\n  const [now, setNow] = useState(null);\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    setNow(new Date().toLocaleTimeString());\\n    const id = setInterval(() => setNow(new Date().toLocaleTimeString()), 1000);\\n    return () => clearInterval(id);\\n  }, []);\\n  return <span>{now ?? \'--:--:--\'}</span>;\\n}\\n\\n// A component that should not be rendered on the server\\nimport dynamic from \'next/dynamic\';\\nconst Chart = dynamic(() => import(\'./Chart\'), { ssr: false });"],[3365,"During the server rendering `localStorage` is unavailable, so the server and the client can draw different HTML and React will issue a hydration error. The solution is to render neutral content on the server and on the first client render, and to substitute the data from `localStorage` after the mounting, through a `useEffect` and state. Thus the first client render matches the server\'s, while the update happens after the hydration.","With server rendering (SSR) or static generation (SSG) the HTML is formed on the server, where the `window` and `localStorage` objects do not exist. During the hydration React compares the server\'s HTML with what the client renders on its first pass. If the client reads localStorage at once and prints a different value, the trees do not match — a hydration mismatch arises.\\nWhy the problem arises\\n\\n- The server does not know about the user\'s data in `localStorage`.\\n\\n- The first client render has to match the server\'s HTML.\\n\\n- Reading `localStorage` directly in the component\'s body breaks that match.\\n\\nThe right approach\\nYou have to separate two states: the initial one (the same on the server and on the client) and the updated one (after the mounting). The initial one is always rendered, while the real data is substituted in a `useEffect`, which runs only on the client after the hydration.\\n\\nWhy this works\\nThe first client render returns the same neutral content as the server, so the hydration passes without errors. After the mounting the `useEffect` reads localStorage and calls `setState`, which leads to an ordinary client-side re-render — now without a comparison with the server.\\nIn Next.js you can use `dynamic(() => import(\'./Comp\'), { ssr: false })` or a wrapper component, which renders the children only after `mounted`. That is the same principle: the server\'s HTML contains no client-side data.\\nIn summary: apply this trick whenever the content depends on `localStorage`, on cookies, on `window` or on other browser APIs — that is the only reliable way of avoiding a hydration mismatch in SSR/SSG applications.","function ThemeLabel() {\\n  const [theme, setTheme] = useState(null); // the same on the server and on the client\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // it runs only on the client, after the hydration\\n    setTheme(localStorage.getItem(\'theme\') || \'light\');\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  if (theme === null) return <span>Loading...</span>; // the neutral content\\n  return <span>The theme: {theme}</span>;\\n}"],[3366,"In the App Router the components are server ones by default, while the `\\"use client\\"` directive marks a file as a client one, enabling the hooks and the browser APIs. The `\\"use server\\"` directive marks functions as Server Actions, which can be called from the client. In the Pages Router there is no such separation: all of the pages are rendered on the server but run as client components. That is, the App Router introduces an explicit server/client boundary, while the Pages Router does not.","In the Next.js App Router (the `app/` folder) all of the components are React Server Components by default. That means they run only on the server, do not reach the client bundle and cannot use hooks such as `useState` or the browser APIs. To mark a file as a client one, the `\\"use client\\"` directive is written at the very top. Then the component and all of its imports become part of the client bundle.\\nThe \\"use client\\" directive\\nIt is placed as the file\'s first line and tells the bundler: this module and its dependencies have to run in the browser. Inside you can use hooks, event handlers, `window`, `localStorage` and so on.\\nThe \\"use server\\" directive\\nIt marks a function as a Server Action — a server-side function that can be called directly from a client component. Next.js itself creates the network call and serializes the arguments. In a client component it can be passed into a form\'s `action` or called by hand.\\n\\nThe difference from the Pages Router\\nIn the Pages Router (the `pages/` folder) there are no Server Components. Any page is an ordinary React component, which is rendered on the server on the first request and then hydrated in the browser. The server-side functions are done through `getServerSideProps` or API routes rather than through directives. That is, the server/client boundary there is implicit and is set by the framework\'s functions rather than by directives in the code.\\nThe practical rules\\n\\n- By default write server components — they are lighter and safer.\\n\\n- Place `\\"use client\\"` only where hooks, events or the browser APIs are needed.\\n\\n- Client components can be nested inside server ones, but not the other way round without an import.\\n\\n- Use `\\"use server\\"` for the mutation of data and for forms.\\n\\nIn summary: the `\\"use client\\"` and `\\"use server\\"` directives are a way of stating the runtime environment explicitly in the App Router, which gives a smaller bundle and better security. In the Pages Router there is no such model, so when migrating it is worth reconsidering which components really do have to be client ones.","// app/Counter.tsx\\n\\"use client\\";\\nimport { useState } from \'react\';\\n\\nexport default function Counter() {\\n  const [n, setN] = useState(0);\\n  return <button onClick={() => setN(n + 1)}>{n}</button>;\\n}\\n\\n// app/actions.ts\\n\\"use server\\";\\nexport async function saveUser(formData) {\\n  const name = formData.get(\'name\');\\n  await db.users.create({ name });\\n}"],[3367,"In Next.js the state is divided into local (`useState`, `useReducer`), global client-side (Context, Redux, Zustand, Jotai) and server-side (React Query, SWR, Server Components). The local state lives inside a component, the global state between the components on the client, and the server state is cached and synchronized with the backend. It matters to take SSR into account: the global stores have to hydrate properly, or there will be a desynchronization between the server and the client. For data from an API, React Query or SWR is used more often, and for UI state Zustand or Context.","Next.js is a framework on top of React, which adds server rendering (SSR), static generation (SSG) and Server Components. Because of that, the management of state here is more complex than in an ordinary SPA: part of the code runs on the server, part on the client, and the state has to be carried between them properly.\\nThe levels of state\\n\\n- Local state — `useState`, `useReducer`. It suits forms, modals and toggles inside one component.\\n\\n- Global client-side state — the Context API, Redux Toolkit, Zustand, Jotai, Recoil. It is needed for the theme, the authorization, the cart, the UI settings.\\n\\n- Server state — React Query, SWR, RTK Query. It caches the data from an API and manages the loading, the errors and the revalidation.\\n\\n- State in the URL — `searchParams`, route params. It is ideal for filters, pagination and tabs, so that the state is preserved on a reload and when a link is shared.\\n\\nThe particulars of SSR and of the hydration\\nWith server rendering the HTML is formed on the server and then \\"comes to life\\" on the client. If a global store is initialized differently on the server and on the client, React will issue a hydration error. Stores such as Redux therefore have to be created afresh for every request, and the initial state has to be passed in through props or through special wrappers.\\n\\nThe practical choice of a tool\\n\\n- Data from an API — React Query or SWR: they cache and revalidate it themselves and work with SSR through prefetching.\\n\\n- Simple global UI state — Zustand or Context: a minimum of boilerplate.\\n\\n- Complex business logic and a big team — Redux Toolkit with RTK Query.\\n\\n- State that has to be in the link — `searchParams` rather than a store.\\n\\nIn Server Components state is not needed at all: they are rendered on the server and have no access to the hooks. The client-side state lives only in the components with the `\'use client\'` directive.\\nIn summary: in Next.js it is worth combining the approaches — give the server state to React Query or to Server Components, keep the global client state in Zustand or Redux, and use the local and the URL state where they are appropriate. Such a mix gives predictability, good performance and the absence of hydration errors.","// app/providers.tsx (the Next.js App Router)\\n\'use client\';\\nimport { Provider } from \'react-redux\';\\nimport { store } from \'./store\';\\n\\nexport function Providers({ children }) {\\n  return <Provider store={store}>{children}</Provider>;\\n}\\n\\n// store.ts\\nimport { configureStore } from \'@reduxjs/toolkit\';\\nimport cartReducer from \'./cartSlice\';\\n\\nexport const store = configureStore({\\n  reducer: { cart: cartReducer },\\n});"]]');export{e as default};